// Narayaneeyam Quiz Data — 3 questions per Dasakam, drawn from the Narayaneeyam text
// Populated: Dasakams 1-40. Append new dasakams in order; the UI groups by `n`.
//
//   n   dasakam number        kv  key verse, e.g. "8.6"
//   t   title                 vs  that verse in Devanagari
//   bh  Bhagavata canto       vt  its translation
//
// A question may carry its own kv/vs/vt to override the dasakam's, for when it
// asks about a different verse. See tools/fetch-verses.py for the source text.

const DASAKAMS = [
{n:1,t:"Glory of the Lord",kv:"1.1",
vt:"That Brahman — of the nature of concentrated bliss and pure consciousness, incomparable, free of the limits of time and space, eternally liberated, illumined by a hundred thousand Vedic texts, unclear to mere sight yet the supreme goal of human life — that very Truth shines directly here in Guruvāyūr. What fortune for its people!",
vs:"सान्द्रानन्दावबोधात्मकमनुपमितं कालदेशावधिभ्यां निर्मुक्तं नित्यमुक्तं निगमशतसहस्रेण निर्भास्यमानम् । अस्पष्टं दृष्टमात्रे पुनरुरुपुरुषार्थात्मकं ब्रह्म तत्वं तत्तावद्भाति साक्षाद् गुरुपवनपुरे हन्त भाग्यं जनानाम् ॥१॥",
qs:[
{q:"Verse 1.1 heaps several descriptions on Brahman. Which one does Bhattathiri place first, as the poem's opening word?",o:["Sāndrānanda — concentrated bliss","Nityamukta — eternally liberated","Nirbhāsyamāna — illumined by scripture","Urupuruṣārtha — the supreme human goal"],a:0,exp:"The poem begins 'sāndrānandāvabodhātmakam' — of the nature of dense, concentrated bliss and pure awareness. Bhattathiri opens not with a name or an invocation but with a definition of the Absolute."},
{q:"What does Dasakam 1 compare the Lord at Guruvāyūr to?",o:["A lamp that never needs replenishing","The pārijāta, granting even what is unasked","A river that carries away all impurity","A mountain sheltering those beneath it"],a:1,kv:"1.8",vs:"नम्राणां सन्निधत्ते सततमपि पुरस्तैरनभ्यर्थितान प्यर्थान् कामानजस्रं वितरति परमानन्दसान्द्रां गतिं च । इत्थं निश्शेषलभ्यो निरवधिकफलः पारिजातो हरे त्वं क्षुद्रं तं शक्रवाटीद्रुममभिलषति व्यर्थमर्थिव्रजोऽयम् ॥८॥",vt:"To those who bow You give constantly, even the things they have not asked of You, and unceasingly bestow the goal that is dense with supreme bliss. Thus, O Hari, You are the pārijāta tree that is within the reach of all and whose fruit has no limit.",exp:"'Anabhyarthitān apy arthān... pārijāto' — to those who merely bow He gives even the things they never asked for, and the goal dense with bliss besides. A wish-tree grants what is asked of it; Bhattathiri says this one goes further."},
{q:"To what does Dasakam 1 compare pure sattva?",o:["A flame burning in a windless place","A reflection in a perfectly still pool","A wave rising on the ocean of Brahman","A seed holding the whole tree within"],a:2,kv:"1.4",vs:"निष्कम्पे नित्यपूर्णे निरवधिपरमानन्दपीयूषरूपे निर्लीनानेकमुक्तावलिसुभगतमे निर्मलब्रह्मसिन्धौ । कल्लोलोल्लासतुल्यं खलु विमलतरं सत्त्वमाहुस्तदात्मा कस्मान्नो निष्कलस्त्वं सकल इति वचस्त्वत्कलास्वेव भूमन् ॥४॥",vt:"On that flawless ocean of Brahman — unshaken, ever full, of the nature of boundless supreme bliss, most lovely with the rows of liberated ones merged in it — they call pure sattva the very likeness of the play of a wave. And of that is Your form.",exp:"'Nirmala-brahma-sindhau kallolollāsa-tulyaṁ khalu vimalataraṁ sattvam' — on the unshaken, ever-full ocean of Brahman, pure sattva is like the play of a wave. And it is of that sattva, he says, that the Lord's own form is made."}
]},
{n:2,t:"Formful Aspect of the Lord",kv:"2.1",
vt:"I worship Your form: the crown rivalling the sun, the brow bright with tilaka, eyes brimming with compassion, a tender smile, shapely nostrils, a pair of makara earrings glinting on the cheeks, the Kaustubha blazing at the throat — resplendent with forest garland, necklaces and the Śrīvatsa.",
vs:"सूर्यस्पर्धिकिरीटमूर्ध्वतिलकप्रोद्भासिफालान्तरं कारुण्याकुलनेत्रमार्द्रहसितोल्लासं सुनासापुटम् । गण्डोद्यन्मकराभकुण्डलयुगं कण्ठोज्वलत्कौस्तुभं त्वद्रूपं वनमाल्यहारपटलश्रीवत्सदीप्रं भजे ॥१॥",
qs:[
{q:"In what order does Dasakam 2 describe the Lord's form?",o:["From the feet upward","Beginning at the heart","In no fixed order","From the crown downward"],a:3,exp:"Bhattathiri follows the keśādi-pādānta method — starting at the crown that rivals the sun and moving down through brow, eyes, smile, throat and chest. Dasakam 2 covers the upper body; the description continues downward through the dasakam."},
{q:"What do the Lord's four arms bear in Dasakam 2?",o:["Mace, conch, discus and lotus","Bow, arrow, sword and shield","Lotus, rosary, book and pot","Trident, drum, noose and goad"],a:0,kv:"2.2",vs:"केयूराङ्गदकङ्कणोत्तममहारत्नाङ्गुलीयाङ्कितश्रीमद्बाहुचतुष्कसङ्गतगदाशङ्खारिपङ्केरुहाम् । काञ्चित् काञ्चनकाञ्चिलाञ्च्छितलसत्पीताम्बरालम्बिनीमालम्बे विमलाम्बुजद्युतिपदां मूर्तिं तवार्तिच्छिदम् ॥२॥",vt:"I take refuge in that spotless form of Yours: the four glorious arms marked with armlets, bracelets and a great jewelled ring, holding mace, conch, discus and lotus, and the shining yellow silk that hangs below the golden girdle.",exp:"'Gadā-śaṅkhāri-paṅkeruhām' — mace, conch, discus and lotus, in four arms marked with armlets, bracelets and a great jewelled ring, above the golden girdle and the hanging yellow silk."},
{q:"Dasakam 2 weighs three paths. Which does Bhattathiri call far the best?",o:["Karma-yoga, the doing of one's own duty","Bhakti, born of love for His beauty","Jñāna, the knowledge the Upaniṣads give","Renunciation of all action altogether"],a:1,kv:"2.7",vs:"एवंभूततया हि भक्त्यभिहितो योगस्स योगद्वयात् कर्मज्ञानमयात् भृशोत्तमतरो योगीश्वरैर्गीयते । सौन्दर्यैकरसात्मके त्वयि खलु प्रेमप्रकर्षात्मिका भक्तिर्निश्रममेव विश्वपुरुषैर्लभ्या रमावल्लभ ॥७॥",vt:"Because Your form is such, that yoga which is called devotion is sung by the lords of yoga as far higher than the two yogas made of works and of knowledge. For in You, whose very nature is the one savour of beauty, devotion consisting in the excess of love arises of itself.",exp:"'Bhaktyabhihito yogas sa yoga-dvayāt karma-jñānamayāt bhṛśottamataro yogīśvarair gīyate' — the lords of yoga sing devotion as far above both the yoga of works and the yoga of knowledge. The reason he gives is beauty: love follows on it of itself."}
]},
{n:3,t:"Suppliant Devotee",kv:"3.10",
vt:"What use in saying more? Until Your compassion arises, O Lord, I shall pass my days at the feet set before me — openly, to the best of my strength, offering prostration, praise and service, whatever my many afflictions make me cry out.",
vs:"किमुक्तैर्भूयोभिस्तव हि करुणा यावदुदियादहं तावद्देव प्रहितविविधार्तप्रलपितः । पुरः क्लृप्ते पादे वरद तव नेष्यामि दिवसान्यथाशक्ति व्यक्तं नतिनुतिनिषेवा विरचयन् ॥१०॥",
qs:[
{q:"How does Dasakam 3 open — what does it say of Your devotees?",o:["They renounce the world and go to the forest","They perform sacrifices without desiring fruit","They chant His names, recall His form, delight in Him","They study the Vedas under a strict teacher"],a:2,kv:"3.1",vs:"पठन्तो नामानि प्रमदभरसिन्धौ निपतिताः स्मरन्तो रूपं ते वरद कथयन्तो गुणकथाः । चरन्तो ये भक्तास्त्वयि खलु रमन्ते परममूनहं धन्यान् मन्ये समधिगतसर्वाभिलषितान् ॥१॥",vt:"Reciting Your names, fallen into an ocean of overwhelming joy, remembering Your form, O giver of boons, telling over the tales of Your qualities — those devotees who so live take delight in You indeed. Supremely blessed are they; and I…",exp:"'Paṭhanto nāmāni... smaranto rūpaṁ te... kathayanto guṇa-kathāḥ' — reciting the names, remembering the form, telling over the tales of His qualities, they fall into an ocean of joy. Bhattathiri then asks only to be counted among them."},
{q:"What three things does he vow to go on offering until the Lord's compassion comes?",o:["Fasting, silence and pilgrimage","Study, sacrifice and charity","Wealth, family and reputation","Prostration, praise and service"],a:3,exp:"'Nati-nuti-niṣevā viracayan' — making obeisance, singing praise, and rendering service, 'yathāśakti', to the extent of his strength. Notably he does not promise to stop complaining, only to keep serving while he does."},
{q:"What does Bhattathiri ask for in preference to liberation throughout Dasakam 3?",o:["Devotion itself, and the health to continue it","Final release from the whole round of rebirth","Lasting fame as a poet among learned men","Wealth enough to provide for his household"],a:0,kv:"3.5",vs:"भवद्भक्तिः स्फीता भवतु मम सैव प्रशमयेदशेषक्लेशौघं न खलु हृदि सन्देहकणिका । न चेद्व्यासस्योक्तिस्तव च वचनं नैगमवचो भवेन्मिथ्या रथ्यापुरुषवचनप्रायमखिलम् ॥५॥",vt:"Let devotion to You grow abundant in me — that alone would quell the whole flood of my afflictions; there is not a grain of doubt of it in my heart. Were it not so, the word of Vyāsa and Your own word and the utterance of the Vedas would all be false, no better than the talk of men in the street.",exp:"The dasakam is the poem's statement of purpose: he wants bhakti, not mokṣa, and enough bodily soundness to keep worshipping. The request for health is not incidental — the whole Narayaneeyam is a prayer offered from illness."}
]},
{n:4,t:"Progress of the Spiritual Aspirant",kv:"4.1",bh:"Bhāgavata II.2",
vt:"Grant me that much soundness of body by which worship of You becomes possible; that, growing strong through the clearly-set practice of the eightfold yoga, I may swiftly attain Your favour.",
vs:"कल्यतां मम कुरुष्व तावतीं कल्यते भवदुपासनं यया । स्पष्टमष्टविधयोगचर्यया पुष्टयाशु तव तुष्टिमाप्नुयाम् ॥१॥",
qs:[
{q:"What exactly does Bhattathiri ask for in the opening verse of Dasakam 4?",o:["Wealth enough to endow a temple","Only as much health as worship requires","Complete freedom from all disease","A long life of a hundred years"],a:1,exp:"'Kalyatāṁ mama kuruṣva tāvatīm' — that much soundness, and no more: precisely the measure by which 'bhavad-upāsanam' becomes possible. He asks for health as a means to worship, never as an end."},
{q:"Which discipline does Dasakam 4 then set out in detail?",o:["The four Vedas and their six auxiliaries","The daily rituals of temple worship","The eightfold yoga, from yama to samādhi","The sixteen saṁskāras of a householder"],a:2,kv:"4.2",vs:"ब्रह्मचर्यदृढतादिभिर्यमैराप्लवादिनियमैश्च पाविताः । कुर्महे दृढममी सुखासनं पङ्कजाद्यमपि वा भवत्पराः ॥२॥",vt:"Purified by the restraints that begin with steadfast celibacy, and by the observances that begin with ablution, may we who are devoted to You hold firm a comfortable posture — the lotus or another.",exp:"'Aṣṭa-vidha-yoga-caryayā' — the eight-limbed path: yama, niyama, āsana, prāṇāyāma, pratyāhāra, dhāraṇā, dhyāna and samādhi, which the dasakam traces through the rising of the vital force to the crown."},
{q:"Dasakam 4 closes on the arcirādi path, the way of light. What does it say of one who goes by it?",o:["He returns once a full kalpa has run its course","He must be born once more to complete the path","He reaches it only after very many lifetimes","He does not fall away — 'vicyutiṁ na bhajate'"],a:3,kv:"4.15",vs:"अर्चिरादिगतिमीदृशीं व्रजन् विच्युतिं न भजते जगत्पते । सच्चिदात्मक भवत् गुणोदयानुच्चरन्तमनिलेश पाहि माम् ॥१५॥",vt:"Going by such a path of light, O Lord of the universe, one does not fall away. O You whose nature is being and consciousness, protect me as I utter the risings of Your qualities, Lord of Anila's city.",exp:"'Arcirādi-gatim īdṛśīṁ vrajan vicyutiṁ na bhajate' — going by the path that begins in light, he does not fall back. Dasakam 4 ends not in despair but in this assurance, with a last request to be kept while uttering the Lord's qualities."}
]},
{n:5,t:"Cosmic Evolution",kv:"5.10",bh:"Bhāgavata II.5",
vt:"That egg lay in the previously created waters for a thousand years. Splitting it open, You made the fourteen-world form called Virāṭ. With thousands of hands, feet and heads, the Self of every living being, You shone forth — O Lord of Marut's city, save me from every affliction.",
vs:"अण्डं तत्खलु पूर्वसृष्टसलिलेऽतिष्ठत् सहस्रं समाः निर्भिन्दन्नकृथाश्चतुर्दशजगद्रूपं विराडाह्वयम् । साहस्रैः करपादमूर्धनिवहैर्निश्शेषजीवात्मको निर्भातोऽसि मरुत्पुराधिप स मां त्रायस्व सर्वामयात् ॥१०॥",
qs:[
{q:"According to this verse, how long did the cosmic egg lie in the waters?",o:["A thousand years","A single night of Brahmā","Twelve years","Until the next kalpa"],a:0,exp:"'Sahasraṁ samāḥ' — a thousand years the aṇḍa rested in the waters brought forth before it, before the Lord entered it and split it open to form the worlds."},
{q:"What is the first evolute to arise when the guṇas of prakṛti are stirred?",o:["Ahaṅkāra, the sense of ego","Mahat, the cosmic intelligence","The five subtle tanmātras","Ākāśa, the element of space"],a:1,kv:"5.4",vs:"मायासन्निहितोऽप्रविष्टवपुषा साक्षीति गीतो भवान् भेदैस्तां प्रतिबिंबतो विविशिवान् जीवोऽपि नैवापरः । कालादिप्रतिबोधिताऽथ भवता संचोदिता च स्वयं माया सा खलु बुद्धितत्त्वमसृजद्योऽसौ महानुच्यते ॥४॥",vt:"You are sung as the witness, close to Māyā yet with a body that never enters it; and as the individual soul You entered her by reflection through her divisions, being yet no other. Then, awakened by Time and the rest and impelled by You, that Māyā created the principle of intellect — the one that is called Mahat.",exp:"Dasakam 5 follows the Sāṅkhya sequence: from disturbed prakṛti comes mahat, from mahat ahaṅkāra, and from the threefold ahaṅkāra the mind and deities, the senses, and the subtle then gross elements."},
{q:"What does the dasakam identify as the cause that sets this whole unfolding in motion?",o:["The spontaneous desire of the elements to combine","Brahmā's will, exercised before the worlds arose","Time, as the Lord's own power, agitating the guṇas","Chance disturbance within the balance of prakṛti"],a:2,kv:"5.3",vs:"एवं च द्विपरार्धकालविगतावीक्षां सिसृक्षात्मिकां बिभ्राणे त्वयि चुक्षुभे त्रिभुवनीभावाय माया स्वयम् । मायातः खलु कालशक्तिरखिलादृष्टं स्वभावोऽपि च प्रादुर्भूय गुणान्विकास्य विदधुस्तस्यास्सहायक्रियाम् ॥३॥",vt:"And so, a dviparārdha having passed, as You bore the glance that is the will to create, Māyā herself was stirred towards becoming the three worlds. From Māyā indeed came forth the power of Time, all unseen destiny, and inherent nature — and unfolding the guṇas, they performed the work of assisting her.",exp:"The equilibrium of the guṇas is disturbed by kāla — time — which Bhattathiri treats not as an independent force but as the Lord's own śakti. Creation is therefore never mechanical; it is His act throughout."}
]},
{n:6,t:"Cosmos as Form of the Lord",kv:"6.1",bh:"Bhāgavata II.1, II.6",
vt:"Thus, of You who have become the fourteen worlds, they call Pātāla the sole of Your foot; the region above the foot is Rasātala, and Your two ankles Mahātala, O wondrous One.",
vs:"एवं चतुर्दशजगन्मयतां गतस्य पातालमीश तव पादतलं वदन्ति । पादोर्ध्वदेशमपि देव रसातलं ते गुल्फद्वयं खलु महातलमद्भुतात्मन् ॥१॥",
qs:[
{q:"In the cosmic form of Dasakam 6, which world is the sole of the Lord's foot?",o:["Bhūloka","Satyaloka","Svarga","Pātāla"],a:3,exp:"'Pātālam īśa tava pādatalaṁ vadanti' — the lowest of the worlds is the sole of the foot, and the dasakam then climbs the body upward through the remaining thirteen."},
{q:"Which realm is identified as the head of the cosmic form?",o:["Satyaloka, the highest world","Maharloka, above the three","Bhuvarloka, the mid-region","Janaloka, home of the sages"],a:0,kv:"6.3",vs:"ग्रीवा महस्तव मुखं च जनस्तपस्तु फालं शिरस्तव समस्तमयस्य सत्यम् । एवं जगन्मयतनो जगदाश्रितैरप्यन्यैर्निबद्धवपुषे भगवन्नमस्ते ॥३॥",vt:"Your neck is Mahar, Your face Jana, Your forehead Tapas, and Your head Satya, O You who are all things. Thus, O You whose body is the universe, salutations to You, whose form is bound together by the very worlds that rest within it.",exp:"The mapping runs from Pātāla at the sole to Satyaloka, Brahmā's own realm, at the head — so that the entire vertical structure of the cosmos is one body."},
{q:"For whom does Dasakam 6 say this cosmic form is especially to be remembered?",o:["For renunciates who have abandoned all rites","For those bound to action, as their action ends","For children beginning their study of the Veda","For kings at the moment of their coronation"],a:1,kv:"6.10",vs:"ईदृग्जगन्मयवपुस्तव कर्मभाजां कर्मावसानसमये स्मरणीयमाहुः । तस्यान्तरात्मवपुषे विमलात्मने ते वातालयाधिप नमोऽस्तु निरुन्धि रोगान् ॥१०॥",vt:"They say such a universe-bodied form of Yours is to be remembered by those bound to action, at the time when their action ends. Salutations to You, the pure inner Self of that form — Lord of Vāta's abode, hold back my diseases.",exp:"'Karma-bhājāṁ karmāvasāna-samaye smaraṇīyam āhuḥ' — it is prescribed for those still engaged in works, at the close of their course. The dasakam then ends on the prayer that gives the whole poem its purpose: nirundhi rogān, hold back my diseases."}
]},
{n:7,t:"Brahma Commanded to Create",kv:"7.4",bh:"Bhāgavata II.9",
vt:"There where Māyā never works her changes, where sorrow, anger, delusion, fear and the rest have gone far off, where a stream of dense bliss flows in a form of supreme light — that contemplated abode of Yours stands victorious as Vaikuṇṭha, O Lord.",
vs:"माया यत्र कदापि नो विकुरुते भाते जगद्भ्यो बहिः शोकक्रोधविमोहसाध्वसमुखा भावास्तु दूरं गताः । सान्द्रानन्दझरी च यत्र परमज्योतिःप्रकाशात्मके तत्ते धाम विभावितं विजयते वैकुण्ठरूपं विभो ॥४॥",
qs:[
{q:"What does this verse say about Māyā in Vaikuṇṭha?",o:["She serves the Lord there in person","She rules over the lower regions of it","She never works her changes there at all","She appears only to the unqualified"],a:2,exp:"'Māyā yatra kadāpi no vikurute' — not that Māyā is absent, but that she never modifies anything there. Vaikuṇṭha is defined by the absence of transformation, which is the absence of the very mechanism of bondage."},
{q:"What did Brahmā hear from the sky when he could not proceed with creation?",o:["A hymn naming the Lord's qualities","An instruction to search the waters","The sound of the conch being blown","The single word 'tapa' — perform penance"],a:3,kv:"7.2",vs:"सोऽयं विश्वविसर्गदत्तहृदयः सम्पश्यमानः स्वयं बोधं खल्वनवाप्य विश्वविषयं चिन्ताकुलस्तस्थिवान् । तावत्त्वं जगतां पते तप तपेत्येवं हि वैहायसीं वाणीमेनमशिश्रवः श्रुतिसुखां कुर्वंस्तपःप्रेरणाम् ॥२॥",vt:"He, his heart given to the sending forth of the universe, looking on but not himself attaining any knowledge of it, stood troubled in mind. Then, O Lord of the worlds, You made him hear a voice from the sky, saying only this: 'tapa, tapa' — perform penance.",exp:"'Tapa tapety evaṁ hi vaihāyasīṁ vāṇīm' — a voice from the sky saying only 'tapa, tapa'. He had been given charge of the worlds and found he had no knowledge of them; that one word was the whole of the instruction."},
{q:"What did the Lord assure Brahmā as He touched his hand?",o:["That knowledge would come, and creation not bind","That creation would be finished within a day","That he would never be born again in any world","That the demons would not trouble his work"],a:0,kv:"7.10",vs:"आताम्रे चरणे विनम्रमथ तं हस्तेन हस्ते स्पृशन् बोधस्ते भविता न सर्गविधिभिर्बन्धोऽपि सञ्जायते । इत्याभाष्य गिरं प्रतोष्य नितरां तच्चित्तगूढः स्वयं सृष्टौ तं समुदैरयः स भगवन्नुल्लासयोल्लाघताम् ॥१०॥",vt:"Then, touching with Your hand the hand of him who bowed at Your reddened feet: 'Understanding shall be yours, nor shall any bondage arise from the work of creation' — so speaking and greatly gladdening him, You Yourself, hidden within his mind, carried on the creating.",exp:"'Bodhas te bhavitā na sarga-vidhibhir bandho 'pi sañjāyate' — understanding will be yours, and the business of creating will not bind you. Bhattathiri adds that He then hid within Brahmā's mind and carried the creation on Himself."}
]},
{n:8,t:"Dawn of Creation",kv:"8.6",bh:"Bhāgavata III.8, III.11",
vt:"In Your own form You lay upon Śeṣa, king of serpents, in a world where the waters alone remained — Yourself of the nature of dense bliss, Your being sealed in the sleep of Your own yoga.",
vs:"तवैव वेषे फणिराजि शेषे जलैकशेषे भुवने स्म शेषे । आनन्दसान्द्रानुभवस्वरूपः स्वयोगनिद्रापरिमुद्रितात्मा ॥६॥",
qs:[
{q:"What poetic feature makes this verse famous?",o:["It can be read backwards to yield a second verse entirely","The syllable 'śeṣe' recurs in each line with a different meaning","It contains no finite verb form anywhere in its four lines","Every syllable in it is metrically long, without exception"],a:1,exp:"A śleṣa: 'phaṇi-rāji-śeṣe' (on Śeṣa the serpent), 'jalaika-śeṣe' (where water alone remained), 'bhuvane sma śeṣe' (You lay). One sound, three senses — Bhattathiri the poet at play in the middle of a cosmological account."},
{q:"How does Dasakam 8 measure Brahmā's days and nights?",o:["Each equal to a single human year","Each equal to one revolution of the sun","Each equal to a thousand caturyugas","Each equal to a hundred divine years"],a:2,kv:"8.2",vs:"सोऽयं चतुर्युगसहस्रमितान्यहानि तावन्मिताश्च रजनीर्बहुशो निनाय । निद्रात्यसौ त्वयि निलीय समं स्वसृष्टैर्नैमित्तिकप्रलयमाहुरतोऽस्य रात्रिम् ॥२॥",vt:"He has passed, many times over, days measured by a thousand caturyugas, and nights of the same measure. In those he sleeps, merged in You, while the three worlds too lie dissolved.",exp:"'Caturyuga-sahasramitāny ahāni tāvan-mitāś ca rajanīḥ' — days measured by a thousand caturyugas and nights of the same measure, passed many times over. In those nights he sleeps merged in the Lord, the three worlds dissolved with him."},
{q:"What appeared from the Lord's navel as this sleep ended?",o:["A flame that rose and became the sun of the coming age","The four Vedas in visible form, chanting themselves aloud","A stream that descended and became the heavenly Gaṅgā","A lotus bud holding the dissolved worlds, in which Brahmā appeared"],a:3,kv:"8.10",vs:"ततस्त्वदीयादयि नाभिरन्ध्रादुदञ्चितं किंचन दिव्यपद्मम् । निलीननिश्शेषपदार्थमालासंक्षेपरूपं मुकुलायमानम् ॥१०॥",vt:"Then from that navel-aperture of Yours, O Lord, there rose up a certain divine lotus in bud — the form in which the whole array of dissolved things lay gathered in brief.",exp:"From the navel rose a luminous lotus containing all the worlds that had been withdrawn, and within it the lotus-born Brahmā — who could not find its root, the search that opens the next dasakam."}
]},
{n:9,t:"Brahma's Meditation and Vision",kv:"9.5",bh:"Bhāgavata III.8–9",
vt:"Through a hundred divine years of unwavering meditation, enlightenment dawned on Brahmā. His inner vision beheld the Lord's wondrous form resting upon Ādiśeṣa.",
vs:"शतेन परिवत्सरैर्दृढसमाधिबन्धोल्लसत्प्रबोधविशदीकृतः स खलु पद्मिनीसम्भवः । अदृष्टचरमद्भुतं तव हि रूपमन्तर्दृशा व्यचष्ट परितुष्टधीर्भुजगभोगभागाश्रयम् ॥५॥",
qs:[
{q:"According to Dasakam 9, how long did Brahma meditate before the Lord's form was revealed to him?",o:["A hundred divine years","A single day","Twelve years","Until the end of the kalpa"],a:0,exp:"Bhattathiri says 'śatena parivatsarair' — through a hundred divine years of firm samādhi, Brahmā's understanding was clarified."},
{q:"What did Brahmā do before the vision, and with what result?",o:["Asked the sages, and was told nothing at all","Searched the lotus stalk a hundred years, in vain","Churned the waters and so raised the earth","Waited in silence until the Lord came to him"],a:1,kv:"9.4",vs:"ततः सकलनालिकाविवरमार्गगो मार्गयन् प्रयस्य शतवत्सरं किमपि नैव संदृष्टवान् । निवृत्य कमलोदरे सुखनिषण्ण एकाग्रधीः समाधिबलमादधे भवदनुग्रहैकाग्रही ॥४॥",vt:"Then, going by the whole channel of the stalk and searching, he strove for a hundred years and saw nothing whatever. Turning back and seating himself at ease in the lotus, single-minded, he took up the strength of contemplation, bent on Your grace alone.",exp:"'Prayasya śata-vatsaraṁ kim api naiva sandṛṣṭavān' — going the whole channel of the stalk and striving a hundred years, he saw nothing whatever. Only then did he turn back, settle in the lotus, and fix his mind."},
{q:"What did the Lord grant Brahmā when He appeared to him?",o:["Rulership over the highest of the worlds","Freedom from every further birth to come","Power to create, and a charge for more penance","A vision of all the ages still to come"],a:2,kv:"9.8",vs:"लभस्व भुवनत्रयीरचनदक्षतामक्षतां गृहाण मदनुग्रहं कुरु तपश्च भूयो विधे । भवत्वखिलसाधनी मयि च भक्तिरत्युत्कटेत्युदीर्य गिरमादधा मुदितचेतसं वेधसम् ॥८॥",vt:"'Receive undiminished skill in the fashioning of the three worlds; accept My grace; and perform penance again, O Creator. And let devotion to Me in you, which accomplishes everything, be exceedingly intense' — so uttering, You made the Creator glad at heart.",exp:"'Labhasva bhuvana-trayī-racana-dakṣatām... kuru tapaś ca bhūyo vidhe' — receive undiminished skill in fashioning the three worlds, accept My grace, and perform penance again. A third follows: let devotion to Me in you be exceedingly intense."}
]},
{n:10,t:"Diversification of Creation",kv:"10.10",bh:"Bhāgavata III.12, III.20",
vt:"Knowing then the means, the unborn one divided his body and took on male and female nature as Manu and his consort. Multiplying the races of men through those two — O Govinda, Lord of Māruta's city, hold back my diseases.",
vs:"जानन्नुपायमथ देहमजो विभज्य स्रीपुंसभावमभजन्मनुतद्वधूभ्याम् । ताभ्यां च मानुषकुलानि विवर्धयंस्त्वं गोविन्द मारुतपुरेश निरुन्धि रोगान् ॥१०॥",
qs:[
{q:"In Dasakam 10, into whom did Brahma divide his own body to further creation?",o:["Marichi and Atri","Sanaka and Sanandana","Daksha and Asikni","Manu and Śatarūpā"],a:3,exp:"Brahmā divided himself into male and female principles, appearing as Svāyambhuva Manu and his consort Śatarūpā, from whom humanity descends."},
{q:"Why did Brahma's mind-born sons Sanaka, Sanandana, Sanatana and Sanatkumara not assist in creation?",o:["They chose renunciation and refused to procreate","They were cursed by their father and lost the power","They were destroyed by the demons of that age","They departed at once for Vaikuṇṭha and never returned"],a:0,kv:"10.3",vs:"तावत् ससर्ज मनसा सनकं सनन्दं भूय: सनातनमुनिं च सनत्कुमारम् । ते सृष्टिकर्मणि तु तेन नियुज्यमानास्त्वत्पादभक्तिरसिका जगृहुर्न वाणीम् ॥३॥",vt:"Then he brought forth from his mind Sanaka and Sananda, and further the sage Sanātana and Sanatkumāra. But appointed by him to the work of creation, they — who relished only devotion at Your feet — would not accept his word.",exp:"The four Kumāras, devoted to nivṛtti (the path of withdrawal), declined Brahmā's command to procreate, choosing lifelong celibacy and contemplation instead."},
{q:"Who arose from Brahma's anger when creation did not proceed as he wished?",o:["Indra","Rudra","Yama","Agni"],a:1,kv:"10.4",vs:"तावत् प्रकोपमुदितं प्रतिरुन्धतोऽस्य भ्रूमध्यतोऽजनि मृडो भवदेकदेश: । नामानि मे कुरु पदानि च हा विरिञ्चेत्यादौ रुरोद किल तेन स रुद्रनामा ॥४॥",vt:"Then, as he checked the anger that had risen in him, Mṛḍa was born from the middle of his brow — a portion of Yourself. 'Give me names, and stations too, O Viriñca!' Thus he wailed at the outset, and by that he became known as Rudra.",exp:"Frustrated at the Kumāras' refusal, Brahmā's anger took form as Rudra, who was then instructed to create — but produced fierce beings and was asked to perform penance instead."}
]},
{n:11,t:"Prelude to the First Incarnation",kv:"11.1",bh:"Bhāgavata III.15–16",
vt:"As creation flourished, the divine sages Sanaka and his brothers came to Vaikuṇṭha to behold the Lord — O Lord of Guruvāyūr.",
vs:"क्रमेण सर्गे परिवर्धमाने कदापि दिव्याः सनकादयस्ते । भवद्विलोकाय विकुण्ठलोकं प्रपेदिरे मारुतमन्दिरेश ॥१॥",
qs:[
{q:"Who came to Vaikuṇṭha to behold the Lord, only to be turned away at the gate?",o:["Nārada and Tumburu together","The ten Pracetas princes","Sanaka and his brother sages","Brahmā and Śiva in company"],a:2,exp:"The verse names 'sanakādayaḥ' — Sanaka, Sanandana, Sanātana and Sanatkumāra, the four eternally youthful mind-born sons of Brahmā, who came simply 'bhavad-vilokāya', to look upon the Lord."},
{q:"What curse did the gatekeepers Jaya and Vijaya receive for barring the sages?",o:["To lose their posts at Vaikuṇṭha","To be turned to stone","To forget the Lord's name","To be born on earth as demons"],a:3,kv:"11.4",vs:"वैकुण्ठलोकानुचितप्रचेष्टौ कष्टौ युवां दैत्यगतिं भजेतम् । इति प्रशप्तौ भवदाश्रयौ तौ हरिस्मृतिर्नोऽस्त्विति नेमतुस्तान् ॥४॥",vt:"'You two, whose conduct befits not the world of Vaikuṇṭha — wretched ones, go to the state of demons!' So they were cursed; and those two, who had taken refuge in You, prayed only that the remembrance of Hari might not desert them.",exp:"Affronted at being stopped, the Kumāras cursed the two doorkeepers to fall from Vaikuṇṭha and be born in demonic wombs — the Lord Himself confirming the curse and promising to restore them."},
{q:"Why does Bhattathiri place this episode before the avatāra narratives?",o:["Because the demons of the coming dasakams are Jaya and Vijaya reborn","Because it explains how the Vedas first came into being","Because it settles the geography of Vaikuṇṭha once for all","Because it introduces the four Kumāras as world-teachers"],a:0,kv:"11.6",vs:"प्रसाद्य गीर्भिः स्तुवतो मुनीन्द्राननन्यनाथावथ पार्षदौ तौ । संरम्भयोगेन भवैस्त्रिभिर्मामुपेतमित्यात्तकृपं न्यगादीः ॥६॥",vt:"Then, appeasing with words the great sages who praised You, You showed Your mercy to those two attendants who had no other lord, saying: 'through three births, by the way of enmity, you shall come to Me.'",exp:"The curse sets the whole avatāra cycle in motion. Jaya and Vijaya return as Hiraṇyākṣa and Hiraṇyakaśipu, then Rāvaṇa and Kumbhakarṇa, then Śiśupāla and Dantavakra — slain each time by the Lord, which is their release."}
]},
{n:12,t:"Varāha Avatāra — Rescue of the Earth",kv:"12.9",bh:"Bhāgavata III.13",
vt:"Locating the earth placed in the depths of Rasātala by the wicked demon, You, O Lord in Boar form, disregarded the rushing demons and lifted her upon the tip of Your tusk, as though in play.",
vs:"दृष्ट्वाऽथ दैत्यहतकेन रसातलान्ते संवेशितां झटिति कूटकिटिर्विभो त्वम् । आपातुकानविगणय्य सुरारिखेटान् दंष्ट्राङ्कुरेण वसुधामदधाः सलीलम् ॥९॥",
qs:[
{q:"From where did the Boar form first emerge in Dasakam 12?",o:["From the cosmic ocean","From Brahmā's nostril","From a cleft in the earth","From the Lord's navel lotus"],a:1,kv:"12.3",vs:"हा हा विभो जलमहं न्यपिबं पुरस्तादद्यापि मज्जति मही किमहं करोमि । इत्थं त्वदङ्घ्रियुगलं शरणं यतोऽस्य नासापुटात् समभवः शिशुकोलरूपी ॥३॥",vt:"'Alas, alas, O Lord — before this I drank up the waters, and even now the earth is sinking; what am I to do?' Thus he took refuge at Your feet — and from his nostril there came forth a boar-form, small as a young animal.",exp:"The Varāha appeared from Brahmā's nostril, at first no bigger than a thumb, then swelling in an instant to a mountainous form that astonished Brahmā and the sages watching."},
{q:"How did Varāha raise the earth from Rasātala?",o:["Cradled in both His arms","Balanced on His shoulders","Upon the tip of His tusk","By a word of command alone"],a:2,exp:"'Daṁṣṭrāṅkureṇa vasudhām adadhāḥ' — He bore the earth up on the sprout-like tip of His tusk, the image Bhattathiri returns to as the signature of this avatāra."},
{q:"How did the Boar form change after it emerged?",o:["It stayed no larger than a young animal","It shone brightly but did not alter in size","It took on a human shape above the neck","Thumb-sized at first, then vast as an elephant"],a:3,kv:"12.4",vs:"अङ्गुष्ठमात्रवपुरुत्पतितः पुरस्तात् भोयोऽथ कुम्भिसदृशः समजृम्भथास्त्वम् । अभ्रे तथाविधमुदीक्ष्य भवन्तमुच्चै र्विस्मेरतां विधिरगात् सह सूनुभिः स्वैः ॥४॥",vt:"Risen up before him with a body no bigger than a thumb, You then swelled to the likeness of an elephant; and seeing You so in the sky, the Creator, together with his sons, fell to wondering greatly.",exp:"'Aṅguṣṭha-mātra-vapur utpatitaḥ... kumbhi-sadṛśaḥ samajṛmbhathāḥ' — risen no bigger than a thumb, then swelling to the bulk of an elephant, and in the next verse to the size of a mountain. Brahmā and his sons watched in astonishment."}
]},
{n:13,t:"Destruction of Hiranyāksha",kv:"13.1",bh:"Bhāgavata III.17–19",
vt:"While Hiraṇyākṣa roamed in search of You through the waters of dissolution — which reached only up to his shanks — Your devotee Nārada, of cleverly deceptive wit, approached him and spoke softly, delighting the demon while belittling Your strength.",
vs:"हिरण्याक्षं तावद्वरद भवदन्वेषणपरं चरन्तं सांवर्ते पयसि निजजङ्घापरिमिते । भवद्भक्तो गत्वा कपटपटुधीर्नारदमुनिः शनैरूचे नन्दन् दनुजमपि निन्दंस्तव बलम् ॥१॥",
qs:[
{q:"What detail does the verse give to convey Hiraṇyākṣa's enormous size?",o:["The waters of dissolution reached only up to his shanks","He could hold whole mountains in the palm of one hand","His shadow was enough to darken all three of the worlds","He stood taller than Mount Meru at its highest peak"],a:0,exp:"'Sāṁvarte payasi nija-jaṅghā-parimite' — the very waters of universal dissolution came only up to his shins as he waded through them, hunting for the Lord."},
{q:"What did Nārada tell Hiraṇyākṣa, and what followed?",o:["That the gods had fled, and he gave chase","That Viṣṇu was carrying off his earth","That his brother had been slain by a boar","That a sacrifice was being held without him"],a:1,kv:"13.2",vs:"स मायावी विष्णुर्हरति भवदीयां वसुमतीं प्रभो कष्टं कष्टं किमिदमिति तेनाभिगदितः । नदन् क्वासौ क्वासविति स मुनिना दर्शितपथो भवन्तं सम्प्रापद्धरणिधरमुद्यन्तमुदकात् ॥२॥",vt:"'That trickster Viṣṇu is carrying off your earth, O Lord — alas, alas, what is this!' Told this by him, and roaring 'where is he, where is he', shown the road by the sage, he came upon You bearing the earth.",exp:"'Sa māyāvī viṣṇur harati bhavadīyāṁ vasumatīm' — that trickster Viṣṇu is taking away your earth. Roaring 'where is he, where is he', and shown the road by the sage, the demon came upon the Lord bearing the earth on His tusk."},
{q:"How did the Lord finally kill Hiraṇyākṣa?",o:["The demon fled at last to the depths of Pātāla","Brahmā intervened and brought the fight to an end","With the big toe of His foot, at the ear's root","The demon was bound in chains and imprisoned"],a:2,kv:"13.7",vs:"भवच्चक्रज्योतिष्कणलवनिपातेन विधुते ततो मायाचक्रे विततघनरोषान्धमनसम् । गरिष्ठाभिर्मुष्टिप्रहृतिभिरभिघ्नन्तमसुरं स्वपादाङ्गुष्ठेन श्रवणपदमूले निरवधीः ॥७॥",vt:"Then, when the wheel of his magic was undone by the fall of the least spark from Your discus, the demon — his mind blind with swelling rage, striking at You with heavy blows of his fists — You slew with the big toe of Your foot, at the root of his ear.",exp:"'Sva-pādāṅguṣṭhena śravaṇa-pada-mūle niravadhīḥ' — with the big toe of His own foot, at the base of the ear. The fists in this verse are the demon's, raining blows on the Lord; the killing stroke is almost dismissive beside them."}
]},
{n:14,t:"Kardama and Kapila Avatāra",kv:"14.3",bh:"Bhāgavata III.21–24",
vt:"O Lord, You revealed Your form to Kardama — mounted upon Garuḍa, hued like a rain-cloud, a playful lotus in Your hand, Your face radiant with a smile.",
vs:"गरुडोपरि कालमेघक्रमं विलसत्केलिसरोजपाणिपद्मम् । हसितोल्लसिताननं विभो त्वं वपुराविष्कुरुषे स्म कर्दमाय ॥३॥",
qs:[
{q:"How is the Lord's appearance to Kardama described in this verse?",o:["Seated on Ādiśeṣa in the midst of the ocean","Blazing with the light of a thousand rising suns","As a small brahmacārin bearing staff and deerskin","Mounted on Garuḍa, cloud-hued, a lotus in His hand"],a:3,exp:"Bhattathiri gives the whole picture in one line: 'garuḍopari', 'kāla-megha', the 'keli-saroja' — the lotus twirled in play — and 'hasitollasitānanam', the face lit by a smile."},
{q:"Whom did Kardama marry, as arranged after this vision?",o:["Devahūti, daughter of Svāyambhuva Manu","Śatarūpā, the consort of Manu himself","Prasūti, who was given to Dakṣa","Merudevī, later the wife of Nābhi"],a:0,kv:"14.6",vs:"मनुनोपहृतां च देवहूतिं तरुणीरत्नमवाप्य कर्दमोऽसौ । भवदर्चननिवृतोऽपि तस्यां दृढशुश्रूषणया दधौ प्रसादम् ॥६॥",vt:"And Kardama, receiving Devahūti, that jewel among young women, brought to him by Manu — though content already in worshipping You — showed her his favour for her steadfast service.",exp:"The Lord directed Manu to give his daughter Devahūti to the sage. From their union came nine daughters, married to the great sages, and finally the Lord Himself as their son Kapila."},
{q:"What did the Lord promise Kardama on this occasion?",o:["Sovereignty over the whole of the earth","That He would be born as Kardama's own son","Immortality and freedom from all disease","A vision of Vaikuṇṭha before his death"],a:1,kv:"14.4",vs:"स्तुवते पुलकावृताय तस्मै मनुपुत्रीं दयितां नवापि पुत्रीः । कपिलं च सुतं स्वमेव पश्चात् स्वगतिं चाप्यनुगृह्य निर्गतोऽभूः ॥४॥",vt:"To him, praising You with his hair standing on end, You granted Manu's daughter as his wife and nine daughters besides, and Kapila — Your own self — as his son, and afterwards Your own state as well; and then You departed.",exp:"The boon was the avatāra itself: the Lord would take birth as Kapila, son of Kardama and Devahūti — and it is that son who delivers the teaching of the next dasakam to his own mother."}
]},
{n:15,t:"Teaching of Kapila",kv:"15.1",bh:"Bhāgavata III.25–33",
vt:"The mind attached to the guṇas binds; unattached to them it grants immortality. Bhakti-yoga restrains that attachment, and bhakti itself is won through following the great. Thus, as Kapila, You taught Devahūti.",
vs:"मतिरिह गुणसक्ता बन्धकृत्तेष्वसक्ता त्वमृतकृदुपरुन्धे भक्तियोगस्तु सक्तिम् । महदनुगमलभ्या भक्तिरेवात्र साध्या कपिलतनुरिति त्वं देवहूत्यै न्यगादीः ॥१॥",
qs:[
{q:"To whom does Kapila deliver this teaching?",o:["To Kardama, his father","To the four Kumāras","To Devahūti, his mother","To Svāyambhuva Manu"],a:2,exp:"'Devahūtyai nyagādīḥ' — the Lord as Kapila taught His own mother. The Sāṅkhya of the Bhāgavata is given not to an assembly of sages but as a son's instruction to his mother."},
{q:"How many principles does Kapila enumerate in Dasakam 15?",o:["Twenty-four, ending with the mind","Sixteen, with the elements as first","Thirty-two, counting the senses twice","Twenty-five, the puruṣa being the last"],a:3,kv:"15.2",vs:"प्रकृतिमहदहङ्काराश्च मात्राश्च भूतान्यपि हृदपि दशाक्षी पूरुषः पञ्चविंशः । इति विदितविभागो मुच्यतेऽसौ प्रकृत्या कपिलतनुरिति त्वं देवहूत्यै न्यगादीः ॥२॥",vt:"Prakṛti, mahat and ahaṅkāra, the subtle elements and the gross elements, the mind too, and the ten senses, with puruṣa as the twenty-fifth — knowing this division, one is freed from prakṛti. Thus, in the body of Kapila, You taught Devahūti.",exp:"'Prakṛti-mahad-ahaṅkārāś ca mātrāś ca bhūtāny api hṛd api daśākṣī pūruṣaḥ pañcaviṁśaḥ' — prakṛti, mahat, ahaṅkāra, the subtle and gross elements, the mind and the ten senses, with puruṣa as the twenty-fifth. Knowing that division, one is freed."},
{q:"What does Kapila call the conqueror of death?",o:["Supreme devotion, from hearing and singing","The knowledge that the self is unborn","The vow of silence kept for a lifetime","The renunciation of every desire at once"],a:0,kv:"15.5",vs:"मम गुणगणलीलाकर्णनैः कीर्तनाद्यैर्मयि सुरसरिदोघप्रख्यचित्तानुवृत्तिः । भवति परमभक्तिः सा हि मृत्योर्विजेत्री कपिलतनुरिति त्वं देवहूत्यै न्यगादीः ॥५॥",vt:"By hearing and by singing and the rest of the multitude of My qualities and sports, there comes to be in Me a flowing of the mind like the stream of the celestial river. That is supreme devotion, and it indeed is the conqueror of death. Thus, in the body of Kapila, You taught Devahūti.",exp:"'Bhavati parama-bhaktiḥ sā hi mṛtyor vijetrī' — from hearing and singing His qualities the mind flows to Him as the celestial river flows to the sea, and that supreme devotion is the conqueror of death."}
]},
{n:16,t:"Daksha's Daughters and Sati",kv:"16.1",bh:"Bhāgavata IV.1–7",
vt:"Dakṣa, son of Brahmā, obtained Prasūti the daughter of Manu, and through her sixteen daughters. Thirteen he gave to Dharma, Svadhā to the Pitṛs, Svāhā to Agni, and Satī to Śiva — who is Your own portion.",
vs:"दक्षो विरिञ्चतनयोऽथ मनोस्तनूजां लब्ध्वा प्रसूतिमिह षोडश चाप कन्याः । धर्मे त्रयोदश ददौ पितृषु स्वधां च स्वाहां हविर्भुजि सतीं गिरिशे त्वदंशे ॥१॥",
qs:[
{q:"How many daughters does the verse say Dakṣa had through Prasūti?",o:["Ten","Sixteen","Thirteen","Twenty-seven"],a:1,exp:"'Ṣoḍaśa cāpa kanyāḥ' — sixteen daughters, of whom thirteen went to Dharma, and the remaining three to the Pitṛs, to Agni, and to Śiva."},
{q:"What happened when Kāma and his company came to disturb the Lord?",o:["They were driven off by a curse from Him","They succeeded, and He withdrew to the mountains","They were themselves bewildered by His maidservants","They were turned to stone where they stood"],a:2,kv:"16.7",vs:"सम्मोहनाय मिलिता मदनादयस्ते त्वद्दासिकापरिमलैः किल मोहमापुः । दत्तां त्वया च जगृहुस्त्रपयैव सर्वस्वर्वासिगर्वशमनीं पुनरुर्वशीं ताम् ॥७॥",vt:"Madana and the others, gathered to bewilder, were themselves bewildered by the very fragrance of Your maidservants; and what You gave them they took in shame — Urvaśī, who quells the pride of all the dwellers in heaven.",exp:"'Madanādayas te tvad-dāsikā-parimalaiḥ kila moham āpuḥ' — Madana and the rest, come to bewilder, were bewildered by the very fragrance of His maidservants. He then gave them Urvaśī, who quells the pride of heaven, and they took her in shame."},
{q:"How does Dasakam 16 end its account of Dakṣa?",o:["Dakṣa was killed outright and never restored to life","Śiva forgave Dakṣa without any confrontation at all","The sacrifice was completed wholly undisturbed","His sacrifice crushed, beheaded, then restored to life"],a:3,kv:"16.10",vs:"क्रुद्धेशमर्दितमखः स तु कृत्तशीर्षो देवप्रसादितहरादथ लब्धजीवः । त्वत्पूरितक्रतुवरः पुनराप शान्तिं स त्वं प्रशान्तिकर पाहि मरुत्पुरेश ॥१०॥",vt:"His sacrifice crushed by the angered Lord and his head struck off, he then had his life again from Hara, whom the gods had appeased; and his sacrifice being completed by You, he regained his peace.",exp:"'Kruddheśa-mardita-makhaḥ sa tu kṛtta-śīrṣo deva-prasādita-harād atha labdha-jīvaḥ' — his sacrifice crushed by the angered Lord and his head struck off, he had his life again from Hara whom the gods appeased. Bhattathiri names neither Vīrabhadra nor the goat's head; those belong to the fuller Purāṇic telling."}
]},
{n:17,t:"Story of Dhruva",kv:"17.1",bh:"Bhāgavata IV.8–12",
vt:"Suruci was the dearly beloved wife of King Uttānapāda, son of Manu. The other, Sunīti, was disregarded by her husband — and she, being without any other recourse, took refuge in You alone.",
vs:"उत्तानपादनृपतेर्मनुनन्दनस्य जाया बभूव सुरुचिर्नितरामभीष्टा । अन्या सुनीतिरिति भर्तुरनादृता सा त्वामेव नित्यमगतिः शरणं गताऽभूत् ॥१॥",
qs:[
{q:"How does the verse describe Sunīti's turning to the Lord?",o:["Being 'agatiḥ' — without recourse — she took refuge in Him","She sought Him after long study of the scriptures","She was instructed to do so by the sage Nārada","She worshipped Him only for the sake of a son"],a:0,exp:"'Agatiḥ śaraṇaṁ gatā' — having nowhere else to go, she went to the only refuge there is. Bhattathiri lingers on this because the Lord is the resort precisely of those with no other resort."},
{q:"Who gave the child Dhruva the mantra for his penance?",o:["His mother Sunīti","Nārada","Brahmā","Uttānapāda's family priest"],a:1,kv:"17.4",vs:"आकर्ण्य सोऽपि भवदर्चननिश्चितात्मा मानी निरेत्य नगरात् किल पञ्चवर्षः । सन्दृष्टनारदनिवेदितमन्त्रमार्गस्त्वामारराध तपसा मधुकाननान्ते ॥४॥",vt:"Hearing that, he too — his mind set on worshipping You, and proud — left the city, though but five years old; and was shown the way of the mantra declared to him by Nārada, whom he met.",exp:"Meeting the five-year-old on his way to the forest, Nārada first tested his resolve, then gave him the twelve-syllable mantra 'oṁ namo bhagavate vāsudevāya' and the method of meditating on the Lord's form."},
{q:"What did the Lord do when He finally appeared before Dhruva?",o:["He granted the boon at once without speaking at all","He carried the boy straight to Vaikuṇṭha with Him","He touched Dhruva's cheek with His conch, and speech came","He restored him immediately to his father's throne"],a:2,kv:"17.7",vs:"त्वद्दर्शनप्रमदभारतरङ्गितं तं दृग्भ्यां निमग्नमिव रूपरसायने ते । तुष्टूषमाणमवगम्य कपोलदेशे संस्पृष्टवानसि दरेण तथाऽऽदरेण ॥७॥",vt:"Knowing him to be thrilled with the joy of beholding You, as though drowned by his two eyes in the elixir of Your beauty, and longing to praise You — You touched him upon the cheek.",exp:"Dhruva, overwhelmed, could not speak. The Lord touched his cheek with the conch — and hymns poured from the child. He was granted the polestar, the one fixed point around which the heavens turn."}
]},
{n:18,t:"Story of Prithu",kv:"18.1",bh:"Bhāgavata IV.13–23",
vt:"To Aṅga, born in the lineage of the illustrious Dhruva, was born a son named Vena. Distressed in mind by that son's wickedness, the noble king withdrew to the forest with his thoughts fixed on Your feet.",
vs:"जातस्य ध्रुवकुल एव तुङ्गकीर्तेरङ्गस्य व्यजनि सुतः स वेननामा । यद्दोषव्यथितमतिः स राजवर्यस्त्वत्पादे निहितमना वनं गतोऽभूत् ॥१॥",
qs:[
{q:"Why did King Aṅga abandon his kingdom for the forest?",o:["He was defeated in battle and lost his kingdom","He was cursed by a sage he had failed to honour","He wished to perform a great sacrifice in peace","He was grieved by the wickedness of his son Vena"],a:3,exp:"'Yad-doṣa-vyathita-matiḥ' — his mind pained by his son's evil, he left throne and city, his thoughts placed on the Lord's feet. Bhattathiri notes the irony: a righteous father, a monstrous son."},
{q:"From what was Pṛthu born, after the sages destroyed Vena?",o:["From the churning of Vena's arms","From a great sacrificial fire","From the body of the earth herself","From the ashes of Vena's pyre"],a:0,kv:"18.4",vs:"तन्नाशात् खलजनभीरुकैर्मुनीन्द्रैस्तन्मात्रा चिरपरिरक्षिते तदङ्गे । त्यक्ताघे परिमथितादथोरुदण्डाद्दोर्दण्डे परिमथिते त्वमाविरासीः ॥४॥",vt:"At his destruction, the great sages — fearing the lawless — churned the body that his mother had long preserved; and when its sin had been shed from the churned thigh, then, as the arm was churned, You appeared.",exp:"The sages, having slain the tyrant with their mantras, churned his thigh — producing the dark dwarf Niṣāda, who carried off Vena's sin — and then his arms, from which arose Pṛthu and his consort Arci."},
{q:"For what act is King Pṛthu chiefly remembered in this dasakam?",o:["Conquering all three of the worlds in a single reign","Milking the earth, in cow form, to yield grain for his people","Building the first city ever raised upon the earth","Composing the hymn by which the Lord is still praised"],a:1,kv:"18.6",vs:"भूयस्तां निजकुलमुख्यवत्सयुक्त्यैर्देवाद्यैः समुचितचारुभाजनेषु । अन्नादीन्यभिलषितानि यानि तानि स्वच्छन्दं सुरभितनूमदूदुहस्त्वम् ॥६॥",vt:"Then, taking as calves the chief one of each of their own kinds, the gods and the rest milked from her in her cow-form, into fitting and beautiful vessels, whatever foods and other things they desired.",exp:"When the earth withheld her produce, Pṛthu pursued her with drawn bow; she took cow form and pleaded, and he then milked her for grain and sustenance. From him she takes the name Pṛthvī."}
]},
{n:19,t:"Story of the Prachetas",kv:"19.1",bh:"Bhāgavata IV.24–31",
vt:"Pṛthu's great-grandson Prācīnabarhis, diligent in the great duties, begot upon his youthful wife Śatadruti ten good-hearted sons named the Pracetas — like sprouts of Your compassion.",
vs:"पृथोस्तु नप्ता पृथुधर्मकर्मठः प्राचीनबर्हिर्युवतौ शतद्रुतौ । प्रचेतसो नाम सुचेतसः सुतानजीजनत्त्वत्करुणाङ्कुरानिव ॥१॥",
qs:[
{q:"To what does the verse compare the ten Pracetas?",o:["To ten flames of a single fire","To the ten directions","To sprouts of the Lord's compassion","To rivers flowing to the sea"],a:2,exp:"'Tvat-karuṇāṅkurān iva' — as though they were shoots springing from the Lord's own compassion. The image tells you in advance that these sons exist for a purpose of grace."},
{q:"Where did the Pracetas perform their long penance?",o:["On a high peak of the Himālaya","In a cave deep beneath the earth","In the great forest of Naimiṣa","Submerged in the waters of the ocean"],a:3,kv:"19.4",vs:"स्तवं जपन्तस्तममी जलान्तरे भवन्तमासेविषतायुतं समाः । भवत्सुखास्वादरसादमीष्वियान् बभूव कालो ध्रुववन्न शीघ्रता ॥४॥",vt:"Chanting that hymn, these worshipped You within the waters for ten thousand years; and through the relish of tasting the bliss of You, so long a span did not seem long to them, as it did not to Dhruva.",exp:"They entered the sea and stood in penance beneath its waters for ten thousand years, holding the Lord in meditation, until He appeared and granted them their boons."},
{q:"Whom did the Pracetas meet on their way, and what did he give them?",o:["Śiva, who taught them the hymn known as the Rudra-gīta","Nārada, who gave them a mantra for their penance","Brahmā, who blessed their journey to the ocean","Kapila, who taught them the Sāṅkhya doctrine"],a:0,kv:"19.3",vs:"तदा भवत्तीर्थमिदं समागतो भवो भवत्सेवकदर्शनादृतः । प्रकाशमासाद्य पुरः प्रचेतसामुपादिशत् भक्ततमस्तव स्तवम् ॥३॥",vt:"Then Bhava, who honours the sight of those who serve You, came to this sacred place of Yours; and appearing before the Pracetas, he — Your foremost devotee — taught them a hymn of praise to You.",exp:"Śiva appeared to the young princes out of grace and taught them the Rudra-gīta, the hymn to Viṣṇu with which they sustained their underwater tapas — one more instance of Bhattathiri showing Śiva as the Lord's foremost devotee."}
]},
{n:20,t:"Story of Ṛṣabha Deva",kv:"20.1",bh:"Bhāgavata V.1–6",
vt:"From King Āgnīdhra, the beloved son of Priyavrata, was born Nābhi. Performing sacrificial rites for Your satisfaction alone, he beheld You in the midst of the sacrifice — the granter of what is desired.",
vs:"प्रियव्रतस्य प्रियपुत्रभूतादाग्नीध्रराजादुदितो हि नाभिः । त्वां दृष्टवानिष्टदमिष्टिमध्ये तवैव तुष्ट्यै कृतयज्ञकर्मा ॥१॥",
qs:[
{q:"In what circumstance did King Nābhi behold the Lord?",o:["In a dream on the night before the rite","In the midst of the sacrifice he was performing","While wandering alone in the deep forest","At the very moment of his own death"],a:1,exp:"'Iṣṭi-madhye' — in the very middle of the rite. And the verse is careful about his motive: 'tavaiva tuṣṭyai', he performed it for the Lord's satisfaction alone, not to obtain anything."},
{q:"What boon did the Lord grant King Nābhi?",o:["Sovereignty over all the continents","Freedom from rebirth","That He would be born as Nābhi's son","A thousand years of life"],a:2,kv:"20.2",vs:"अभिष्टुतस्तत्र मुनीश्वरैस्त्वं राज्ञः स्वतुल्यं सुतमर्थ्यमानः । स्वयं जनिष्येऽहमिति ब्रुवाणस्तिरोदधा बर्हिषि विश्वमूर्ते ॥२॥",vt:"Praised there by the lords of sages, and asked by the king for a son equal to Yourself, You said 'I Myself shall be born' — and, O Form of the universe, vanished from the sacrificial altar.",exp:"The Lord, pleased with the disinterested worship, promised to incarnate as his son — and was born to Nābhi and Merudevī as Ṛṣabhadeva."},
{q:"After whom is Bhārata-varṣa named, according to this dasakam?",o:["Ṛṣabha himself, the avatāra of that age","King Nābhi, who first beheld the Lord","Priyavrata, ancestor of the whole line","Bharata, the eldest of Ṛṣabha's hundred sons"],a:3,kv:"20.6",vs:"जितेन्द्रदत्तां कमनीं जयन्तीमथोद्वहन्नात्मरताशयोऽपि । अजीजनस्तत्र शतं तनूजानेषां क्षितीशो भरतोऽग्रजन्मा ॥६॥",vt:"Then, wedding the lovely Jayantī given by Indra, though Your delight was in the Self alone, You begot there a hundred sons — of whom Bharata, the first-born, was lord of the earth.",exp:"Ṛṣabhadeva instructed his hundred sons, gave the kingdom to the eldest, Bharata, and took to the life of an avadhūta. It is from that Bharata that this land takes its name."}
]},
{n:21,t:"Worship in Different Continents",kv:"21.1",bh:"Bhāgavata V.16–25",
vt:"The poet seeks refuge in the Lord who resides as Saṅkarṣaṇa in the Ilāvṛta region, worshipped by Śiva with sacred hymns.",
vs:"मध्योद्भवे भुव इलावृतनाम्नि वर्षे गौरीप्रधानवनिताजनमात्रभाजि । शर्वेण मन्त्रनुतिभिः समुपास्यमानं सङ्कर्षणात्मकमधीश्वर संश्रये त्वाम् ॥१॥",
qs:[
{q:"In the Ilāvṛta region described in Dasakam 21, which form of the Lord is worshipped, and by whom?",o:["Saṅkarṣaṇa, worshipped by Śiva","Nṛsiṃha, worshipped by Prahlāda","Hayagrīva, worshipped by Bhadraśravas","Kūrma, worshipped by Aryamā"],a:0,exp:"The opening verse states that in Ilāvṛta — where Gaurī and her companions dwell — the Lord abides as Saṅkarṣaṇa, worshipped by Śarva (Śiva) with mantras and hymns of praise."},
{q:"Dasakam 21 surveys the varṣas of Jambūdvīpa. In Kimpuruṣa-varṣa, who is described as the Lord's worshipper?",o:["Nārada, worshipping Nara-Nārāyaṇa","Hanumān, worshipping Śrī Rāma","Manu, worshipping the Fish form","Bhūmi Devī, worshipping Varāha"],a:1,kv:"21.8",vs:"याम्यां दिशं भजति किंपुरुषाख्यवर्षे संसेवितो हनुमता दृढभक्तिभाजा । सीताभिरामपरमाद्भुतरूपशाली रामात्मकः परिलसन् परिपाहि विष्णो ॥८॥",vt:"Turning to the southern quarter, in the region called Kimpuruṣa, You are served by Hanumān of steadfast devotion — shining there as Rāma, of surpassing and wondrous beauty beside Sītā.",exp:"Bhattathiri describes Hanumān as eternally worshipping Śrī Rāma in Kimpuruṣa-varṣa — one of the nine regions where a distinct form of the Lord is adored."},
{q:"In Bhārata-varṣa, how is the Lord worshipped according to Dasakam 21?",o:["As Saṅkarṣaṇa, attended by the serpent kings","As Hayagrīva, restoring the hidden Vedas","As Nārāyaṇa, the friend of Nara, with Nārada","As Nṛsiṁha, in a lofty and white form"],a:2,kv:"21.9",vs:"श्रीनारदेन सह भारतखण्डमुख्यैस्त्वं साङ्ख्ययोगनुतिभिः समुपास्यमानः । आकल्पकालमिह साधुजनाभिरक्षी नारायणो नरसखः परिपाहि भूमन् ॥९॥",vt:"In Bhārata-khaṇḍa You are worshipped, with Śrī Nārada and the foremost of that land, through Sāṅkhya, yoga and hymns of praise — Nārāyaṇa, the friend of Nara, guarding the good here until the end of the kalpa.",exp:"'Śrī-nāradena saha bhārata-khaṇḍa-mukhyaiḥ... nārāyaṇo nara-sakhaḥ' — worshipped there with Sāṅkhya, yoga and hymns, guarding the good until the end of the kalpa. The other three forms named belong to other varṣas of this same dasakam."}
]},
{n:22,t:"Story of Ajamila",kv:"22.1",bh:"Bhāgavata VI.1–2",
vt:"A virtuous brāhmaṇa named Ajāmila, while on an errand to the forest at his father's behest, encountered an immoral woman of loose character.",
vs:"अजामिलो नाम महीसुरः पुरा चरन् विभो धर्मपथान् गृहाश्रमी । गुरोर्गिरा काननमेत्य दृष्टवान् सुधृष्टशीलां कुलटां मदाकुलाम् ॥१॥",
qs:[
{q:"How does Dasakam 22 describe Ajamila before his fall?",o:["A wicked man from the very hour of his birth","A king who had abandoned his throne and city","A wandering ascetic without settled home","A brāhmaṇa householder walking the paths of dharma"],a:3,exp:"The first verse calls him 'mahīsuraḥ' — a brāhmaṇa — 'caran dharmapathān gṛhāśramī', a householder faithfully treading the ways of righteousness, until the forest encounter undid him."},
{q:"At the moment of death, what act saved Ajamila from the messengers of Yama?",o:["He called out the name of his youngest son, Nārāyaṇa","A lifetime of carefully performed ritual sacrifice","His wife prayed on his behalf at the final hour","He gave away the whole of his wealth in charity"],a:0,kv:"22.3",vs:"स मृत्युकाले यमराजकिङ्करान् भयङ्करांस्त्रीनभिलक्षयन् भिया । पुरा मनाक् त्वत्स्मृतिवासनाबलात् जुहाव नारायणनामकं सुतम् ॥३॥",vt:"At the hour of death, marking the three dreadful servants of Yama, in his fear — by the strength of a faint disposition towards remembering You, formed long before — he called aloud.",exp:"Calling for his beloved youngest son, Ajāmila uttered 'Nārāyaṇa' — and though he meant only the boy, the Name itself summoned the Viṣṇudūtas, who disputed Yama's claim and released him."},
{q:"What did Ajamila do after he was granted a second life?",o:["He returned at once to his former way of living","He passed his days in remembrance and reached the Lord","He became a king and ruled for many years","He remained with his family entirely unchanged"],a:1,kv:"22.10",vs:"इतीरितैर्याम्यभटैरपासृते भवद्भटानां च गणे तिरोहिते । भवत्स्मृतिं कंचन कालमाचरन् भवत्पदं प्रापि भवद्भटैरसौ ॥१०॥",vt:"When at these words the servants of Yama had withdrawn and the host of Your attendants had vanished, he passed some time in the remembrance of You, and was brought by Your attendants to Your abode.",exp:"'Bhavat-smṛtiṁ kañcana kālam ācaran bhavat-padaṁ prāpi' — passing some time in the remembrance of You, he was brought to Your abode by Your own attendants. Bhattathiri does not name Haridvāra; the pilgrimage belongs to the fuller Bhāgavata telling."}
]},
{n:23,t:"Story of Chitraketu",kv:"23.1",bh:"Bhāgavata VI.4–17",
vt:"Dakṣa, son of the Pracetas, worshipped the Lord seeking to multiply progeny, and the Lord appeared with eight resplendent arms, granting him a boon and Asiknī as wife.",
vs:"प्राचेतसस्तु भगवन्नपरो हि दक्षस्त्वत्सेवनं व्यधित सर्गविवृद्धिकामः । आविर्बभूविथ तदा लसदष्टबाहुस्तस्मै वरं ददिथ तां च वधूमसिक्नीम् ॥१॥",
qs:[
{q:"In what form did the Lord appear to Dakṣa Prācetasa at the opening of Dasakam 23?",o:["With four arms, bearing conch and discus","As a brilliant light without form","With eight resplendent arms","As Nṛsiṃha"],a:2,exp:"The verse says 'lasad-aṣṭa-bāhuḥ' — the Lord manifested with eight shining arms, granted Dakṣa his boon, and gave him Asiknī as his wife so that creation might multiply."},
{q:"How did King Chitraketu come to lose the son he had longed for?",o:["The child was killed in battle while still young","The child was taken by a curse laid by Nārada","The child renounced the world and went away","The co-wives, consumed by jealousy, poisoned the child"],a:3,kv:"23.4",vs:"प्राक्शूरसेनविषये किल चित्रकेतुः पुत्राग्रही नृपतिरङ्गिरसः प्रभावात् । लब्ध्वैकपुत्रमथ तत्र हते सपत्नीसङ्घैरमुह्यदवशस्तव माययासौ ॥४॥",vt:"Formerly in the land of Śūrasena, King Chitraketu, longing for a son, obtained one by the power of Aṅgiras; and when that child was killed there by the band of co-wives, helpless, he was bewildered by Your Māyā.",exp:"Chitraketu, childless among many queens, obtained a son through sage Aṅgiras — but the other queens, envious that the child's mother alone was favoured, administered poison and killed him."},
{q:"What did Nārada and Aṅgiras reveal to the grieving Chitraketu?",o:["That the soul has had countless parents, such bonds are brief","That his son would certainly be reborn to him again","That he should perform a great sacrifice for the child","That the guilty queens should be put to death at once"],a:0,kv:"23.5",vs:"तं नारदस्तु सममङ्गिरसा दयालुः सम्प्राप्य तावदुपदर्श्य सुतस्य जीवम् । कस्यास्मि पुत्र इति तस्य गिरा विमोहं त्यक्त्वा त्वदर्चनविधौ नृपतिं न्ययुङ्क्त ॥५॥",vt:"But Nārada, compassionate, coming together with Aṅgiras, showed him the soul of his son; and when at its words — 'whose son am I?' — the king had let go of his delusion, they set him to the worship of You.",exp:"The sages summoned the departed soul, which spoke of its innumerable births and asked which of its countless parents it should now call its own. Freed of grief, Chitraketu received the Saṅkarṣaṇa vidyā and attained the Lord's vision."}
]},
{n:24,t:"Story of Prahlada",kv:"24.1",bh:"Bhāgavata VII.1–5",
vt:"When Hiraṇyākṣa was slain by the Lord in His Boar form, his grief-stricken brother Hiraṇyakaśipu vowed before the demons to kill the Lord.",
vs:"हिरण्याक्षे पोत्रिप्रवरवपुषा देव भवता हते शोकक्रोधग्लपितधृतिरेतस्य सहजः । हिरण्यप्रारम्भः कशिपुरमरारातिसदसि प्रतिज्ञमातेने तव किल वधार्थं मधुरिपो ॥१॥",
qs:[
{q:"What prompted Hiraṇyakaśipu's vow at the start of Dasakam 24?",o:["The theft of his kingdom by Indra and the host of gods","The slaying of his brother Hiraṇyākṣa by the Lord as Varāha","A prophecy foretelling the birth of his destroyer son","A public insult offered to him by Brahmā himself"],a:1,exp:"The verse opens with Hiraṇyākṣa slain by the Lord in the form of the noble Boar. His brother, his composure destroyed by grief and rage, vowed in the demons' assembly to kill the Lord Himself."},
{q:"Where did Prahlada first receive instruction in devotion?",o:["From his court teachers Ṣaṇḍa and Amarka","From his father's own priests at court","In his mother's womb, from the sage Nārada","From Brahmā, who instructed him directly"],a:2,kv:"24.4",vs:"ततोऽस्य प्रह्लादः समजनि सुतो गर्भवसतौ मुनेर्वीणापाणेरधिगतभवद्भक्तिमहिमा । स वै जात्या दैत्यः शिशुरपि समेत्य त्वयि रतिं गतस्त्वद्भक्तानां वरद परमोदाहरणताम् ॥४॥",vt:"Then Prahlāda was born to him as a son, who while yet dwelling in the womb had received from the sage who bears the vīṇā the greatness of devotion to You. A demon by birth, yet even as a child he came to delight in You, and became, O giver of boons, the supreme example among Your devotees.",exp:"While Kayādhu carried him, Nārada taught her the truths of bhakti — and the child in the womb absorbed every word, emerging as a devotee before he could walk."},
{q:"What was the outcome of Hiraṇyakaśipu's repeated attempts to kill his son?",o:["Prahlāda was gravely injured but survived them","Prahlāda fled the kingdom and hid in the forest","The demons refused to carry out their king's orders","Every attempt failed — poison, elephants, fire, the sea"],a:3,kv:"24.7",vs:"स शूलैराविद्धः सुबहु मथितो दिग्गजगणैर्महासर्पैर्दष्टोऽप्यनशनगराहारविधुतः । गिरीन्द्रवक्षिप्तोऽप्यहह! परमात्मन्नयि विभो त्वयि न्यस्तात्मत्वात् किमपि न निपीडामभजत ॥७॥",vt:"Pierced with spears, crushed again and again by the elephants of the quarters, bitten by great serpents, tormented by starvation and by poisoned food, hurled down too from the lord of mountains — ah, Supreme Self, O Lord! — because he had placed his self in You, he suffered no pain at all.",exp:"Poison, trampling elephants, serpents, fire, hurling from cliffs and drowning in the sea all left Prahlāda untouched, his mind absorbed in the Lord throughout — the Lord Himself standing as his protection."}
]},
{n:25,t:"Incarnation as Man-Lion",kv:"25.1",bh:"Bhāgavata VII.8",
vt:"When Hiraṇyakaśipu struck the pillar, a terrifying roar emerged that shook the universe and even caused Brahmā to tremble from his seat.",
vs:"स्तंभे घट्टयतो हिरण्यकशिपोः कर्णौ समाचूर्णयन्नाघूर्णज्जगदण्डकुण्डकुहरो घोरस्तवाभूद्रवः । श्रुत्वा यं किल दैत्यराजहृदये पूर्वं कदाप्यश्रुतं कम्पः कश्चन संपपात चलितोऽप्यम्भोजभूर्विष्टरात् ॥१॥",
qs:[
{q:"According to the opening verse of Dasakam 25, what effect did the roar from the pillar have on Brahma?",o:["He trembled and slipped from his lotus seat","He hastened at once to praise the Lord","He fled from his own seat to Vaikuṇṭha","He alone was unable to hear the sound"],a:0,exp:"Bhattathiri says the roar shook the shell of the cosmic egg, struck an unprecedented tremor into the demon king's heart, and even made the lotus-born Brahmā stir and fall from his seat."},
{q:"How did the Man-Lion form circumvent every clause of Hiraṇyakaśipu's boon?",o:["By striking him down while he lay deep in sleep at night","Neither man nor beast, at twilight, on the threshold, by claws","By using a weapon specially forged for Him by Brahmā","By having Prahlāda himself deliver the killing blow"],a:1,kv:"25.6",vs:"भ्राम्यन्तं दितिजाधमं पुनरपि प्रोद्गृह्य दोर्भ्यां जवात् द्वारेऽथोरुयुगे निपात्य नखरान् व्युत्खाय वक्षोभुवि । निर्भिन्दन्नधिगर्भनिर्भरगलद्रक्ताम्बु बद्धोत्सवं पायं पायमुदैरयो बहु जगत्संहारिसिंहारवान् ॥६॥",vt:"Seizing that vilest of demons once more with both arms as he whirled, and swiftly casting him down at the doorway upon Your two thighs, You drew out Your claws and tore open his breast; and drinking, drinking the blood that streamed from within, in a rapture of festival You raised roar upon lion-roar, as though the world were ending.",exp:"The boon excluded man and beast, day and night, indoors and outdoors, earth and sky, and all weapons. Nṛsiṃha was neither man nor beast, came at dusk, on the doorway, placed him on His lap, and used only His nails."},
{q:"Who finally pacified the Lord's terrible wrath after Hiraṇyakaśipu was slain?",o:["Brahmā, with hymns of careful praise","Lakṣmī, by her own intervention","Prahlāda, approaching in humble devotion","Śiva, in his form as Śarabha"],a:2,kv:"25.9",vs:"भूयोऽप्यक्षतरोषधाम्नि भवति ब्रह्माज्ञया बालके प्रह्लादे पदयोर्नमत्यपभये कारुण्यभाराकुलः । शान्तस्त्वं करमस्य मूर्ध्नि समधाः स्तोत्रैरथोद्गायतस्तस्याकामधियोऽपि तेनिथ वरं लोकाय चानुग्रहम् ॥९॥",vt:"When Your fury was still undiminished, and the boy Prahlāda, at Brahmā's command, bowed at Your feet without fear — overcome with the weight of compassion, You grew calm.",exp:"None of the gods dared approach the blazing Nṛsiṃha. The child Prahlāda came forward and bowed, and at the touch of His devotee's devotion the Lord grew calm and blessed him."}
]},
{n:26,t:"Liberation of the Elephant-king",kv:"26.1",bh:"Bhāgavata VIII.2–4",
vt:"King Indradyumna of the Pāṇḍya kingdom was so deeply absorbed in worshipping the Lord on Malaya mountain that he failed to notice the arrival of sage Agastya.",
vs:"इन्द्रद्युम्नः पाण्ड्यखण्डाधिराजस्त्वद्भक्तात्मा चन्दनाद्रौ कदाचित् । त्वत् सेवायां मग्नधीरालुलोके नैवागस्त्यं प्राप्तमातिथ्यकामम् ॥१॥",
qs:[
{q:"Why was King Indradyumna cursed to be born as an elephant?",o:["He insulted Agastya deliberately before his court","He refused to give the sage any alms at all","He hunted freely in the sage's own forest","Absorbed in worship, he failed to notice Agastya's arrival"],a:3,exp:"The verse is careful on this point: 'tvat-sevāyāṁ magna-dhīḥ' — his mind was drowned in serving the Lord, and so he simply did not see Agastya arrive. The curse fell on an act of devotion, not of malice."},
{q:"After struggling with the crocodile for a thousand years, what did Gajendra finally do?",o:["He lifted a lotus and cried out in surrender to the Lord","He tore himself free by his own great strength","He summoned the other elephants of the herd for help","He resigned himself at last to a certain death"],a:0,kv:"26.7",vs:"आर्तिव्यक्तप्राक्तनज्ञानभक्तिः शुण्डोत्क्षिप्तैः पुण्डरीकैः समर्चन् । पूर्वाभ्यस्तं निर्विशेषात्मनिष्ठं स्तोत्रं श्रेष्ठं सोऽन्वगादीत् परात्मन् ॥७॥",vt:"His devotion and the knowledge of former births made plain by his distress, worshipping with lotuses lifted up in his trunk, [he called upon] the undifferentiated One practised in earlier lives.",exp:"When his own strength was wholly exhausted, Gajendra raised a lotus in his trunk and called upon the Supreme — and the Lord, abandoning even Garuḍa in His haste, came at once."},
{q:"Who was the crocodile that seized Gajendra?",o:["An ordinary beast that lived in that lake","A gandharva named Hūhū, cursed by the sage Devala","A demon sent for the purpose by Hiraṇyakaśipu","A form assumed by Yama to test the elephant"],a:1,kv:"26.5",vs:"हूहूस्तावद्देवलस्यापि शापात् ग्राहीभूतस्तज्जले बर्तमानः । जग्राहैनं हस्तिनं पाददेशे शान्त्यर्थं हि श्रान्तिदोऽसि स्वकानाम् ॥५॥",vt:"Meanwhile Hūhū, by the curse of Devala, had become a crocodile living in that water; and he seized this elephant by the foot — which was for the elephant's own peace.",exp:"The crocodile was the gandharva Hūhū, condemned to that form by sage Devala. Struck by the Lord's discus, he too was released — both captor and captive liberated by the same act of grace."}
]},
{n:27,t:"Churning the Ocean for Nectar",kv:"27.1",bh:"Bhāgavata VIII.5–7",
vt:"Sage Durvāsas gave a divine garland to Indra, but when Indra's elephant trampled it, the enraged sage cursed Indra, for forbearance is rare in those not born of the Lord.",
vs:"दर्वासास्सुरवनिताप्तदिव्यमाल्यं शक्राय स्वयमुपदाय तत्र भूयः । नागेन्द्रप्रतिमृदिते शशाप शक्रं का क्षान्तिस्त्वदितरदेवतांशजानाम् ॥१॥",
qs:[
{q:"What act brought Durvāsas' curse upon Indra?",o:["Indra refused outright the gift the sage brought him","Indra failed to rise from his throne to greet him","Indra's elephant trampled the garland the sage had given him","Indra mocked the sage's appearance before the court"],a:2,exp:"Indra placed the garland on Airāvata, who cast it down and trampled it. Bhattathiri adds a pointed aside — 'kā kṣāntiḥ' — what forbearance can be expected of those not born of the Lord's own portion?"},
{q:"What served as the churning rod and the churning rope for the ocean of milk?",o:["Mount Meru and Ādiśeṣa","Mount Kailāsa and Takṣaka","Mount Malaya and Kāliya","Mount Mandara and Vāsuki"],a:3,kv:"27.5",vs:"आधाय द्रुतमथ वासुकिं वरत्रां पाथोधौ विनिहितसर्वबीजजाले । प्रारब्धे मथनविधौ सुरासुरैस्तैर्व्याजात्त्वं भुजगमुखेऽकरोस्सुरारीन् ॥५॥",vt:"Then, swiftly setting Vāsuki as the churning rope in the ocean into which all seeds had been cast, when the work of churning had been begun by those gods and demons, by a device You placed the enemies of the gods at the serpent's mouth.",exp:"Mandara was uprooted as the churning staff and the serpent Vāsuki became the rope, the devas taking the tail and the asuras insisting on the head — where they were scorched by his breath."},
{q:"When Mount Mandara began sinking into the ocean, how did the Lord intervene?",o:["He took the form of Kūrma and bore it on His back","He held it up above the water with one hand","He commanded Varuṇa to raise it from below","He fixed it in place with the Sudarśana disc"],a:0,kv:"27.6",vs:"क्षुब्धाद्रौ क्षुभितजलोदरे तदानीं दुग्धाब्धौ गुरुतरभारतो निमग्ने । देवेषु व्यथिततमेषु तत्प्रियैषी प्राणैषीः कमठतनुं कठोरपृष्ठाम् ॥६॥",vt:"When the mountain shook and the ocean of milk was churned within, and it sank beneath its exceeding weight, and the gods were in the deepest distress — desiring their good, You took on the body of a tortoise.",exp:"The Lord took the form of a vast tortoise and supported the mountain on His back, at the same time entering the devas and asuras as strength so the churning could continue."}
]},
{n:28,t:"Gifts of the Ocean",kv:"28.1",bh:"Bhāgavata VIII.8",
vt:"The Kālakūṭa poison emerged first from the churning ocean like molten fire, and Śiva drank it to please the Lord after the gods praised him with hymns.",
vs:"गरलं तरलानलं पुरस्ताज्जलधेरुद्विजगाल कालकूटम् । अमरस्तुतिवादमोदनिघ्नो गिरिशस्तन्निपपौ भवत्प्रियार्थम् ॥१॥",
qs:[
{q:"What was the first thing to emerge from the churning ocean?",o:["The goddess Lakṣmī","The Kālakūṭa poison","The wish-fulfilling cow Surabhi","The nectar of immortality"],a:1,exp:"Before any treasure came the Kālakūṭa — 'tarala-analam', like restless fire. Śiva drank it down, moved by the gods' hymns and, as the verse says, to please the Lord Himself."},
{q:"When Lakṣmī arose from the ocean, whom did she choose?",o:["Indra, the king of the gods, then reigning","Bali, the king of the asuras at that time","The Lord Viṣṇu, garlanding Him of her own accord","None of them — she remained with the ocean"],a:2,kv:"28.7",vs:"गिरिशद्रुहिणादिसर्वदेवान् गुणभाजोऽप्यविमुक्तदोषलेशान् । अवमृश्य सदैव सर्वरम्ये निहिता त्वय्यनयाऽपि दिव्यमाला ॥७॥",vt:"Considering all the gods — Śiva, Brahmā and the rest — that though possessed of virtues were not free of the least taint of fault, she was placed upon You alone, who are ever wholly delightful.",exp:"Rising in radiance while gods and demons alike hoped for her, Śrī passed them all by and placed her garland upon the Lord's chest, choosing Him as her eternal abode."},
{q:"Who finally rose from the ocean bearing the pot of nectar?",o:["Varuṇa, lord of the waters","Candra, risen from the sea","Airāvata, the white elephant of Indra","Dhanvantari, physician of the gods"],a:3,kv:"28.10",vs:"तरुणाम्बुदसुन्दरस्तदा त्वं ननु धन्वन्तरिरुत्थितोऽम्बुराशेः । अमृतं कलशे वहन् कराभ्यामखिलार्तिं हर मारुतालयेश ॥१०॥",vt:"Then, beautiful as a fresh rain-cloud, You rose from the ocean as Dhanvantari, bearing the nectar in a pot with both hands — taking away every affliction.",exp:"After Surabhi, Vāruṇī, the Pārijāta tree, the apsarases, Airāvata, Uccaiḥśravas and the Kaustubha gem, Dhanvantari emerged holding the vessel of amṛta — at which the asuras seized it, setting up the next dasakam."}
]},
{n:29,t:"Incarnation as Mohini",kv:"29.1",bh:"Bhāgavata VIII.9, VIII.12",
vt:"When the Asuras snatched the nectar that emerged from the Lord's hands, He consoled the helpless Devas and vanished, causing the demons to quarrel among themselves.",
vs:"उद्गच्छतस्तव करादमृतं हरत्सु दैत्येषु तानशरणाननुनीय देवान् । सद्यस्तिरोदधिथ देव भवत्प्रभावादुद्यत्स्वयूथ्यकलहा दितिजा बभूवुः ॥१॥",
qs:[
{q:"According to the opening verse of Dasakam 29, what happened among the Asuras after they seized the nectar?",o:["They fell to quarrelling among themselves","They drank it at once and became immortal","They hid it in the ocean","They offered it back to the Devas"],a:0,exp:"The Lord consoled the helpless devas and vanished — and by His power the daityas immediately fell into strife with their own kinsmen over who should drink first, buying the time that Mohinī would need."},
{q:"How did Mohinī obtain the pot of nectar from the Asuras?",o:["She seized it from them outright by force of arms","Enchanted by her beauty, they gave it to her to divide","She exchanged it with them for a boon they desired","Garuḍa swooped down and carried it away for her"],a:1,kv:"29.3",vs:"का त्वं मृगाक्षि विभजस्व सुधामिमामित्यारूढरागविवशानभियाचतोऽमून् । विश्वस्यते मयि कथं कुलटाऽस्मि दैत्या इत्यालपन्नपि सुविश्वसितानतानीः ॥३॥",vt:"'Who are you, doe-eyed one? Divide this nectar for us' — so they begged, overcome with risen passion. 'How can you place trust in me? I am a wanton woman...'",exp:"Bewitched by the incomparable woman who appeared before them, the asuras themselves entrusted her with the amṛta and agreed to abide by however she chose to distribute it."},
{q:"Who came asking to see the Mohinī form, having heard of it?",o:["Brahmā, wishing to witness the deception","Indra, seeking to recover the lost nectar","Maheśa, who came with Girijā and the bhūtas","Nārada, curious as ever about Your play"],a:2,kv:"29.8",vs:"योषावपुर्दनुजमोहनमाहितं ते श्रुत्वा विलोकनकुतूहलवान् महेशः । भूतैस्समं गिरिजया च गतः पदं ते स्तुत्वाऽब्रवीदभिमतं त्वमथो तिरोधाः ॥८॥",vt:"Hearing of that woman's form of Yours, appointed for the deluding of the demons, Maheśa, filled with curiosity to see it, went with his hosts and with Girijā to Your abode, and having praised You, told his desire.",exp:"'Śrutvā vilokana-kutūhalavān maheśaḥ, bhūtaiḥ samaṁ girijayā ca gataḥ padaṁ te' — hearing of the form assumed to delude the demons, Śiva came with Pārvatī and his hosts and asked to see it. Rāhu, whom this question used to be about, does not appear in Dasakam 29 at all."}
]},
{n:30,t:"Incarnation as Vamana",kv:"30.1",bh:"Bhāgavata VIII.15–18",
vt:"The noble Asura Bali, slain by Indra but revived by Śukrācārya and empowered by sacrifice, fearlessly conquered the three worlds as the terrified gods went into hiding.",
vs:"शक्रेण संयति हतोऽपि बलिर्महात्मा शुक्रेण जीविततनुः क्रतुवर्धितोष्मा । विक्रान्तिमान् भयनिलीनसुरां त्रिलोकीं चक्रे वशे स तव चक्रमुखादभीतः ॥१॥",
qs:[
{q:"According to the opening verse of Dasakam 30, how did Bali return to power after being slain by Indra?",o:["He was born again as another demon in the next age","Brahmā granted him a boon of complete immortality","He had never in truth been killed by Indra at all","Śukrācārya revived him, and sacrifice increased his might"],a:3,exp:"'Śukreṇa jīvita-tanuḥ kratu-vardhitoṣmā' — brought back to life by Śukrācārya and his power swelled by sacrificial rites, Bali conquered the three worlds while the gods hid in fear."},
{q:"What did Aditi do on the advice of her husband Kaśyapa, to relieve the gods' distress?",o:["She performed the Payovrata in worship of the Lord","She appealed directly to Bali for their release","She sent the gods to Brahmā to plead for them","She undertook a fast unto death in protest"],a:0,kv:"30.2",vs:"पुत्रार्तिदर्शनवशाददितिर्विषण्णा तं काश्यपं निजपतिं शरणं प्रपन्ना । त्वत्पूजनं तदुदितं हि पयोव्रताख्यं सा द्वादशाहमचरत्त्वयि भक्तिपूर्णा ॥२॥",vt:"Aditi, stricken at the sight of her sons' affliction, took refuge in Kaśyapa her husband; and the worship of You that he declared to her — that called the Payovrata — she performed for twelve days, filled with devotion to You.",exp:"Grieving for her sons driven from heaven, Aditi was instructed by Kaśyapa in the Payovrata — a vow of worship sustained on milk — and the Lord, pleased, promised to be born as her own son."},
{q:"How does Dasakam 30 describe Vamana as He set out for Bali's sacrifice?",o:["As a mighty warrior clad in full armour and arms","As a radiant dwarf brahmacārin with staff and deerskin","As an aged ascetic worn thin by long austerity","As a king arriving in his full royal procession"],a:1,kv:"30.6",vs:"तावत्प्रजापतिमुखैरुपनीय मौञ्जीदण्डाजिनाक्षवलयादिभिरर्च्यमानः । देदीप्यमानवपुरीश कृताग्निकार्यस्त्वं प्रास्थिथा बलिगृहं प्रकृताश्वमेधम् ॥६॥",vt:"Then, invested with the sacred thread by Prajāpati and the others and honoured with the muñja girdle, staff, deerskin and rosary, Your body shining, O Lord, the fire-rite performed — You set out for Bali's house, where the horse sacrifice was in progress.",exp:"The Lord appeared as a short-statured brahmacārin whose brilliance nonetheless dimmed the assembly, bearing the umbrella, staff, water-pot and deerskin of a student, and walked to the sacrificial ground of Bali."}
]},
{n:31,t:"Vamana Blessing Bali",kv:"31.1",bh:"Bhāgavata VIII.19–23",
vt:"Delighted at the sight of Your radiant form, the Asura worshipped You in every way, O Unconquered One, and folding his hands said: what do you wish from me? Speak, O son of a brāhmaṇa — wealth, food, a dwelling, or the earth itself, I shall give it all.",
vs:"प्रीत्या दैत्यस्तव तनुमहःप्रेक्षणात् सर्वथाऽपि त्वामाराध्यन्नजित रचयन्नञ्जलिं सञ्जगाद । मत्तः किं ते समभिलषितं विप्रसूनो वद त्वं वित्तं भक्तं भवनमवनीं वाऽपि सर्वं प्रदास्ये ॥",
qs:[
{q:"Bali offers the young brahmacārin anything he names. What does Vāmana ask for?",o:["A share of the merit of Bali's sacrifice","The whole of the earth and its treasuries","Three paces of land, measured by His own foot","A seat among the priests at the ceremony"],a:2,kv:"31.2",vs:"तामीक्षणां बलिगिरमुपाकर्ण्य कारुण्यपूर्णोऽप्यस्योत्सेकं शमयितुमना दैत्यवंशं प्रशंसन् । भूमिं पादत्रयपरिमितां प्रार्थयामासिथ त्वं सर्वं देहीति तु निगदिते कस्य हास्यं न वा स्यात् ॥२॥",vt:"Hearing that speech of Bali, and though full of compassion, minded to humble his conceit, You praised the demon's line and asked for ground measured by three paces — when 'give me everything' had been said, whose laughter would not rise?",exp:"Offered wealth, food, a dwelling or the earth entire — 'vittaṁ bhaktaṁ bhavanam avanīṁ vāpi sarvaṁ pradāsye' — the Lord asks only for as much ground as three of His own small steps will cover. Bali is amused that so little is wanted."},
{q:"Who tried to prevent Bali from granting the request, and how did Bali respond?",o:["Prahlāda warned him, and Bali withdrew the gift","His ministers objected, and Bali overruled them","No one objected, and the gift was made at once","Śukrācārya warned him, but Bali kept his word"],a:3,kv:"31.5",vs:"याचत्येवं यदि स भगवान् पूर्णकामोऽस्मि सोऽहं दास्याम्येव स्थिरमिति वदन् काव्यशप्तोऽपि दैत्यः । विन्ध्यावल्या निजदयितया दत्तपाद्याय तुभ्यं चित्रं चित्रं सकलमपि स प्रार्पयत्तोयपूर्वम् ॥५॥",vt:"'If the Lord asks in this way, then I am one whose desires are fulfilled; I shall certainly give, and stand by it' — so he said; and though cursed by Śukra, the demon, with water poured first by his beloved Vindhyāvalī who gave the offering for Your feet, gave over everything, wonder upon wonder.",exp:"Śukrācārya saw through the disguise and told Bali the boy was Viṣṇu Himself. Bali answered that to break a promise is the greater ruin, and that a gift begged for by the Lord is not a loss — and was cursed by his own preceptor for it."},
{q:"With two strides the Lord had covered earth and heaven. Where did the third fall?",o:["Upon Bali's own head, which he offered himself","Upon the summit of Mount Meru, at the centre","Upon the head of Śukrācārya, who had objected","Nowhere — the Lord withdrew it out of mercy"],a:0,kv:"31.9",vs:"पाशैर्बद्धं पतगपतिना दैत्यमुच्चैरवादीस्तार्त्तीयीकं दिश मम पदं किं न विश्वेश्वरोऽसि । पादं मूर्ध्नि प्रणय भगवन्नित्यकम्पं वदन्तं प्रह्लाद्स्तं स्वयमुपगतो मानयन्नस्तवीत्त्वाम् ॥९॥",vt:"To the demon bound with the cords of the lord of birds You said aloud: 'Show me my third step — are you not lord of the world?' And as he answered without trembling, 'Set Your foot upon my head, O Lord', Prahlāda came of himself and, honouring him, praised You.",exp:"No space remained, and Bali bowed and offered his head for the third step. The Lord set His foot there, gave him rulership of Sutala, and — the turn Bhattathiri dwells on — took up station as the doorkeeper of the demon king He had just dispossessed."}
]},
{n:32,t:"Incarnation as Fish",kv:"32.1",bh:"Bhāgavata VIII.24",
vt:"Long ago, when at the close of the sixth Manvantara an untimely dissolution drew near, the Vedas were carried off by the great Asura Hayagrīva from the mouth of the drowsy Brahmā — and You resolved upon the form of a fish.",
vs:"पुरा हयग्रीवमहासुरेण षष्ठान्तरान्तोद्यदकाण्डकल्पे । निद्रोन्मुखब्रह्ममुखात् हृतेषु वेदेष्वधित्सः किल मत्स्यरूपम् ॥",
qs:[
{q:"From whom did the Asura Hayagrīva steal the Vedas?",o:["From the sages who were reciting them","From the mouth of Brahmā, drowsy with sleep","From the guardians set over the four quarters","From the ocean where they had been hidden"],a:1,exp:"'Nidronmukha-brahma-mukhāt' — from the mouth of Brahmā as he grew heavy with sleep at the close of the sixth Manvantara. The theft is possible only in that moment of inattention."},
{q:"How did the Lord as Matsya first come to King Satyavrata?",o:["As a great fish already filling the ocean","As a voice heard from within the waters","As a tiny fish in the water of his cupped palm","As a fish caught in the nets of his fishermen"],a:2,kv:"32.2",vs:"सत्यव्रतस्य द्रमिलाधिभर्तुर्नदीजले तर्पयतस्तदानीम् । कराञ्जलौ सञ्ज्वलिताकृतिस्त्वमदृश्यथाः कश्चन बालमीनः ॥२॥",vt:"Then, as Satyavrata, lord of the Draviḍa country, was offering water in the river, You appeared in the hollow of his joined hands as a certain little fish, of blazing form.",exp:"A minnow small enough to hold, which begged not to be left where larger fish would devour it — then outgrew pot, pond, lake and river in turn, until Satyavrata understood who had come to him."},
{q:"When the deluge came, how was Satyavrata's boat secured?",o:["It was anchored to the peak of Mount Meru","It was held steady by the seven sages at the oars","It was carried upon the back of Ādiśeṣa himself","The sages tied it to the fish's towering horn"],a:3,kv:"32.7",vs:"झषाकृतिं योजनलक्षदीर्घां दधानमुच्चैस्तरतेजसं त्वाम् । निरीक्ष्य तुष्टा मुनयस्त्वदुक्त्या त्वत्तुङ्गशृङ्गे तरणिं बबन्धुः ॥७॥",vt:"Seeing You bearing the form of a fish a hundred thousand leagues long and of exceedingly high splendour, the sages were glad, and at Your word they tied the boat to Your lofty horn.",exp:"'Tvat-tuṅga-śṛṅge taraṇiṁ babandhuḥ' — at Your word the sages fastened the boat to Your high horn. Bhattathiri does not bring Vāsuki in as the rope; that detail belongs to the Bhāgavata's fuller account, not to this dasakam."}
]},
{n:33,t:"Story of Ambarisha",kv:"33.1",bh:"Bhāgavata IX.4–5",
vt:"Ambarīṣa, son of the king named Nābhāga who was born of Nabhaga son of Vaivasvata Manu — though lord of the earth girdled by the seven seas — ever delighted in Your devotees, his mind always immersed in You.",
vs:"वैवस्वताख्यमनुपुत्रनभागजातनाभागनामकनरेन्द्रसुतोऽम्बरीषः । सप्तार्णवावृतमहीदयितोऽपि रेमे त्वत्सङ्गिषु त्वयि च मग्नमनास्सदैव ॥",
qs:[
{q:"What does the verse contrast with Ambarīṣa's devotion?",o:["His lordship over the sea-girdled earth","His great age and long years of rule","His mastery of the Vedas and their branches","His descent from a long line of sages"],a:0,exp:"'Saptārṇavāvṛta-mahī-dayito 'pi' — although he was lord of the earth ringed by the seven seas. The 'api' is the point: sovereignty of that scale usually leaves no room for anything else, and in him it left everything."},
{q:"How did Ambarīṣa break his Ekādaśī fast while Durvāsas was still absent?",o:["He waited past the hour rather than offend","He took only water, to observe both duties","He ate the full meal at the priests' bidding","He sent servants to bring the sage back at once"],a:1,kv:"33.5",vs:"राज्ञाऽथ पारणमुहूर्तसमाप्तिखेदाद्वारैव पारणमकारि भवत्परेण । प्राप्तो मुनिस्तदथ दिव्यदृशा विजानन् क्षिप्यन् क्रुधोद्धृतजटो विततान कृत्याम् ॥५॥",vt:"Then the king, distressed that the hour for breaking the fast was ending, and devoted to You, broke it with water alone. The sage, arriving and knowing this by his divine sight, hurled abuse; and with his matted hair raised in anger he produced a Kṛtyā.",exp:"The Dvādaśī had to be broken within its hour, yet eating before one's guest is a fault. Taking water alone was held to be both eating and not eating — an attempt to fail neither obligation, which the returning sage nevertheless took as an insult."},
{q:"What happened when Durvāsas sent a demon against the king?",o:["Ambarīṣa's guards destroyed it before it struck","Brahmā appeared and dissolved it with a word","The Sudarśana burnt it and then pursued the sage","The demon turned upon Durvāsas of its own accord"],a:2,kv:"33.6",vs:"कृत्यां च तामसिधरां भुवनं दहन्तीमग्रेऽभिवीक्ष्यनृपतिर्न पदाच्चकम्पे । त्वद्भक्तबाधमभिवीक्ष्य सुदर्शनं ते कृत्यानलं शलभयन् मुनिमन्वधावीत् ॥६॥",vt:"Seeing before him that Kṛtyā bearing a sword and burning the world, the king did not stir from his place. And Your Sudarśana, seeing the harm intended to Your devotee, made a moth of the Kṛtyā's fire and pursued the sage.",exp:"The discus consumed the kṛtyā and then chased Durvāsas through every world. Neither Brahmā nor Śiva could shelter him, and the Lord sent him back to the devotee he had wronged — where the king's own prayer called the weapon off."}
]},
{n:34,t:"Incarnation as Rama",kv:"34.1",bh:"Bhāgavata IX.10",
vt:"Entreated by the gods for the destruction of the ten-faced one, You were born as Rāma — when Ṛṣyaśṛṅga in Kosala performed the sacrifice for offspring and gave King Daśaratha the excellent pāyasa, and by eating it his three queens conceived together — with Lakṣmaṇa, Bharata, and one named Śatrughna.",
vs:"गीर्वाणैरर्थ्यमानो दशमुखनिधनं कोसलेष्वृश्यशृङ्गे पुत्रीयामिष्टिमिष्ट्वा ददुषि दशरथक्ष्माभृते पायसाग्र्यम् । तद्भुक्त्या तत्पुरन्ध्रीष्वपि तिसृषु समं जातगर्भासु जातो रामस्त्वं लक्ष्मणेन स्वयमथ भरतेनापि शत्रुघ्ननाम्ना ॥",
qs:[
{q:"What did the gods entreat the Lord to accomplish by this birth?",o:["The recovery of the stolen Vedas","The lifting of a curse laid on Indra","The restoration of sacrifice on earth","The destruction of the ten-faced Rāvaṇa"],a:3,exp:"'Daśamukha-nidhanam' — the death of the ten-faced one. Bhattathiri names the purpose in the opening breath, before the birth itself, so the whole avatāra is read backwards from Laṅkā."},
{q:"What did Rāma leave with Bharata when he came to the forest?",o:["His sandals, and the earth entrusted with them","His bow, to be kept safe until his return","A written order naming Bharata as the king","Nothing at all — he sent him away unheard"],a:0,kv:"34.6",vs:"श्रुत्वा पुत्रार्तिखिन्नं खलु भरतमुखात् स्वर्गयातं स्वतातं तप्तो दत्वाऽम्बु तस्मै निदधिथ भरते पादुकां मेदिनीं च अत्रिं नत्वाऽथ गत्वा वनमतिविपुलं दण्डकं चण्डकायं हत्वा दैत्यं विराधं सुगतिमकलयश्चारु भोः शारभङ्गीम् ॥६॥",vt:"Hearing from Bharata's mouth that Your father, grieved by sorrow for his son, had gone to heaven, You burned, and gave him the water-offering; then You placed the sandals and the earth in Bharata's charge. Bowing to Atri and going on to the vast Daṇḍaka forest, You slew the fierce-bodied demon Virādha, and granted a fair passage to Śarabhaṅga.",exp:"'Nidadhitha bharate pādukāṁ medinīṁ ca' — he placed the sandals, and the earth itself, in Bharata's charge. Bharata ruled with them set on the throne rather than take it himself."},
{q:"Whom did Rāma restore while travelling with Viśvāmitra?",o:["Śabarī, who had waited many years for Him","Ahalyā, freed from her long petrification","Jaṭāyu, wounded in defence of Sītā","Guha, the chieftain of the forest people"],a:1,kv:"34.3",vs:"मारीचं द्रावयित्वा मखशिरसि शरैरन्यरक्षांसि निघ्नन् कल्यां कुर्वन्नहल्यां पथि पदरजसा प्राप्य वैदेहगेहम् । भिन्दानश्चान्द्रचूडं धनुरवनिसुतामिन्दिरामेव लब्ध्वा राज्यं प्रातिष्ठथास्त्वं त्रिभिरपि च समं भ्रातृवीरैस्सदारैः ॥३॥",vt:"Driving off Mārīca and killing the other demons with arrows at the head of the sacrifice, making Ahalyā whole with the dust of Your feet on the road, and reaching Videha's house — breaking the bow of the moon-crested one and winning the earth's daughter, who is Indirā herself — You set out for Your kingdom with all three brother-heroes and their brides.",exp:"The dust of His feet released Ahalyā from the stone into which a curse had turned her. The episode falls on the road to Mithilā, before the breaking of Śiva's bow won Him Sītā."}
]},
{n:35,t:"Incarnation as Rama (contd.)",kv:"35.1",bh:"Bhāgavata IX.10–11",
vt:"Brought then into friendship with Sugrīva by Hanumān, You flung up the carcass of Dundubhi high with Your toe, and further clove seven Sāla trees at once with a single arrow.",
vs:"नीतस्सुग्रीवमैत्रीं तदनु हनुमता दुन्दुभेः कायमुच्चैः क्षिप्त्वाङ्गुष्ठेन भूयो लुलुविथ युगपत् पत्रिणा सप्त सालान् ॥",
qs:[
{q:"What did Hanumān bring back to Rāma from Laṅkā?",o:["Rāvaṇa's crown, taken from the palace","A message written in Sītā's own hand","Sītā's crest-jewel, given as a token","The ring Rāma had sent, returned unused"],a:2,kv:"35.3",vs:"त्वद्वार्ताकर्णनोद्यद्गरुदुरुजवसम्पातिसम्पातिवाक्यप्रोत्तीर्णार्णोधिरन्तर्नगरि जनकजां वीक्ष्य दत्वाङ्गुलीयम् । प्रक्षुद्योद्यानमक्षक्षपणचणरणः सोढबन्धो दशास्यं दृष्ट्वा प्लुष्ट्वा च लङ्कां झटिति स हनुमान् मौलिरत्नं ददौ ते ॥३॥",vt:"Crossing the ocean by the words of Sampāti — whose wings rose in great speed at hearing news of You — and seeing Jānakī within the city, and giving her the ring; laying waste the grove, famed in battle for the slaying of Akṣa, enduring capture, seeing the ten-faced one and burning Laṅkā — that Hanumān at once gave You her crest-jewel.",exp:"'Maulī-ratnaṁ dadau te' — having shown Sītā the ring, laid waste the grove, endured capture and burnt Laṅkā, he gave Rāma her crest-jewel. The whole errand is compressed into this one verse."},
{q:"What did Rāma do with the carcass of Dundubhi?",o:["Buried it beneath the hill at Sugrīva's word","Burnt it with an arrow drawn from His quiver","Left it untouched as a warning to Vāli","Flung it far into the air with a single toe"],a:3,exp:"'Kāyam uccaiḥ kṣiptvāṅguṣṭhena' — hurled up with the big toe alone. Sugrīva needed proof that this stranger could match Vāli, and the ease of the gesture was itself the proof."},
{q:"Why does Bhattathiri say the Lord took birth as a man at all?",o:["For the instruction of mortals — martya-śikṣārtham","To show the gods the strength of a mortal man","Because a curse had required a human birth","To test whether devotion can survive grief"],a:0,kv:"35.10",vs:"सोऽयं मर्त्यावतारस्तव खलु नियतं मर्त्यशिक्षार्थमेवं विश्लेषार्तिर्निरागस्त्यजनमपि भवेत् कामधर्मातिसक्त्या । नो चेत् स्वात्मानुभूतेः क्व नु तव मनसो विक्रिया चक्रपाणे स त्वं सत्त्वैकमूर्ते पवनपुरपते व्याधुनु व्याधितापान् ॥१०॥",vt:"This mortal descent of Yours was surely for the instruction of mortals: that grief at separation, and even the abandoning of a blameless wife, may follow from excessive attachment to desire and to duty. Were it not so, where could there be any disturbance in the mind of You who rest in the experience of Your own Self, O bearer of the discus?",exp:"'Martya-śikṣārtham evam' — the grief of separation, and even the abandoning of a blameless wife, are set down as instruction for mortals. Otherwise, he asks, where could there be any disturbance in the mind of one who rests in His own bliss?"}
]},
{n:36,t:"Incarnation as Parasurama",kv:"36.1",bh:"Bhāgavata IX.15–16",
vt:"Long ago You were born as the one called Datta, to Atri and Anasūyā — Your mind untroubled by the bonds of disciples, wandering at ease and self-contained, in the company of Your consort.",
vs:"अत्रेः पुत्रतया पुरा त्वमनसूयायां हि दत्ताभिधो जातः शिष्यनिबन्धतन्द्रितमनाः स्वस्थश्चरन् कान्तया ॥",
qs:[
{q:"To which parents was Dattātreya born?",o:["Kaśyapa and Aditi, mother of the gods","Atri and Anasūyā, famed for her chastity","Jamadagni and Reṇukā of the Bhṛgu line","Kardama and Devahūti, in an earlier age"],a:1,exp:"'Atreḥ putratayā... anasūyāyām' — born to the sage Atri and Anasūyā, whose fidelity is proverbial. Bhattathiri notes His manner too: 'svasthaś caran', wandering unbound and at ease in Himself."},
{q:"To whom was Paraśurāma born?",o:["To Atri and Anasūyā, as before","To Kaśyapa and Aditi in a later age","To Jamadagni and Reṇukā, of Bhṛgu's line","To Kārtavīrya's own house, as a son"],a:2,kv:"36.2",vs:"सत्यं कर्तुमथार्जुनस्य च वरं तच्छक्तिमात्रानतं ब्रह्मद्वेषि तदाखिलं नृपकुलं हन्तुं च भूमेर्भरम् । सञ्जातो जमदग्नितो भृगुकुले त्वं रेणुकायां हरे रामो नाम तदात्मजेष्ववरजः पित्रोरधाः सम्मदम् ॥२॥",vt:"Then, to make true the boon of Arjuna, who bowed only to that power of Yours, and to destroy that whole line of kings that hated the brāhmaṇas, and to relieve the burden of the earth, You were born, O Hari, of Jamadagni in the family of Bhṛgu, upon Reṇukā, by the name of Rāma — the youngest of their sons; and You gave joy to Your parents.",exp:"'Sañjāto jamadagnito bhṛgukule tvaṁ reṇukāyāṁ hare' — born of Jamadagni in the family of Bhṛgu, upon Reṇukā, and the youngest of their sons. The same verse names the purpose: to make Arjuna's boon true, and to relieve the earth."},
{q:"How many times did Paraśurāma clear the earth of its kṣatriyas?",o:["Seven times, once in each continent","Twelve times, through twelve long years","Three times, and then laid down his axe","Twenty-one times, in successive campaigns"],a:3,kv:"36.10",vs:"तातोज्जीवनकृन्नृपालककुलं त्रिस्सप्तकृत्वो जयन् सन्तर्प्याथ समन्तपञ्चकमहारक्तहृदौघे पितृन् यज्ञे क्ष्मामपि काश्यपादिषु दिशन् साल्वेन युध्यन् पुनः कृष्णोऽमुं निहनिष्यतीति शमितो युद्धात् कुमारैर्भवान् ॥१०॥",vt:"Conquering thrice seven times over the line of kings that had revived after Your father's restoration, and then satisfying the ancestors at Samantapañcaka in its great lake of blood, and giving the earth away at the sacrifice to Kaśyapa and the others, and fighting again with Sālva — You were restrained from battle by the boy-sages, who said that Kṛṣṇa would slay him.",exp:"Twenty-one times, in vengeance for his father's murder — after which he gave the conquered earth to Kaśyapa and withdrew to Mahendra mountain, the wrath spent."}
]},
{n:37,t:"Prelude to Sri Krishna-Incarnation",kv:"37.1",bh:"Bhāgavata X.1",
vt:"O Lord of dense bliss, Hari — those demons slain by You of old in the wars of gods and Asuras, who yet through the remainder of their karma had not attained the final goal, were born again on earth; and the Earth, sorely afflicted by the burden of these sons of Diti, went to Brahmā for refuge, the gods having come there before her.",
vs:"सान्द्रानन्दतनो हरे ननु पुरा दैवासुरे सङ्गरे त्वत्कृत्ता अपि कर्मशेषवशतो ये ते न याता गतिम् । तेषां भूतलजन्मनां दितिभुवां भारेण दूरार्दिता भूमिः प्राप विरिञ्चमाश्रितपदं देवैः पुरैवागतैः ॥",
qs:[
{q:"Why were the demons slain in earlier wars born again on earth?",o:["Because their remaining karma was unexhausted","Because a boon had promised them return","Because Brahmā granted them a second life","Because the Lord had willed a further test"],a:0,exp:"'Karma-śeṣa-vaśataḥ' — by force of the residue of their karma. Death at the Lord's hand had not finished their account, so they returned; and their return is what makes the descent at Gokula necessary."},
{q:"What did the Lord promise, in the words Brahmā relayed to the gods?",o:["To destroy the demons without taking birth at all","To be born in the Yādava line, the gods in the Vṛṣṇi","To send Śiva to the earth in His own place","To end this age at once and begin another"],a:1,kv:"37.5",vs:"जाने दीनदशामहं दिविषदां भूमेश्च भीमैर्नृपैस्तत्क्षेपाय भवामि यादवकुले सोऽहं समग्रात्मना । देवा वृष्णिकुले भवन्तु कलया देवाङ्गनाश्चावनौ मत्सेवार्थमिति त्वदीयवचनं पाथोजभूरूचिवान् ॥५॥",vt:"'I know the wretched state of the dwellers in heaven, and of the earth, from these terrible kings. To cast them off I shall be born in the Yādava clan, I Myself, in My fullness. Let the gods be born in the Vṛṣṇi line in part, and the women of the gods on earth, for My service' — so the lotus-born one spoke Your words.",exp:"'Bhavāmi yādavakule so 'haṁ samagrātmanā; devā vṛṣṇikule bhavantu kalayā' — I shall be born in the Yādava clan in My fullness; let the gods be born in the Vṛṣṇi line in part, and the women of the gods on earth, for My service."},
{q:"What did the voice from the sky tell Kaṃsa as he drove the wedding chariot?",o:["That the Yadus were gods come down to earth","That his own sister would betray him one day","That the eighth child of Devakī would kill him","That his reign would last a hundred years"],a:2,kv:"37.7",vs:"उद्वाहावसितौ तदीयसहजः कंसोऽथ सम्मानयन्नेतौ सूततया गतः पथि रथे व्योमोत्थया त्वद्गिरा । अस्यास्त्वामतिदुष्टमष्टमसुतो हन्तेति हन्तेरितः सन्त्रासात् स तु हन्तुमन्तिकगतां तन्वीं कृपाणीमधात् ॥७॥",vt:"When the marriage was completed, her own brother Kaṃsa, honouring the pair, went along the road as their charioteer; and by Your voice risen from the sky — 'her eighth son shall kill you, most wicked one' — thus told, he in terror took up the sword at hand to kill the slender woman.",exp:"'Asyās tvām atiduṣṭam aṣṭama-suto hanteti' — her eighth son will kill you, most wicked one. He drew his sword on Devakī where she stood, and was stopped only by Vasudeva's promise to hand over every child."}
]},
{n:38,t:"Birth of Sri Krishna",kv:"38.1",bh:"Bhāgavata X.3",
vt:"O Lord whose form is bliss itself — when the time of Your descent had come, the season of rains shone in splendour, veiling the sky with masses of heavy clouds that seemed the streams of lustre issuing from Your own radiant body.",
vs:"आनन्दरूप भगवन्नयि तेऽवतारे प्राप्ते प्रदीप्तभवदङ्गनिरीयमाणैः । कान्तिव्रजैरिव घनाघनमण्डलैर्द्यामावृण्वती विरुरुचे किल वर्षवेला ॥",
qs:[
{q:"What image does the verse use for the gathering rain clouds?",o:["A herd of dark cattle crossing the sky","The smoke of a thousand sacrificial fires","The wings of Garuḍa spread over the world","Streams of lustre issuing from His own body"],a:3,exp:"'Kānti-vrajair iva ghanāghana-maṇḍalaiḥ' — the massed clouds as though they were floods of radiance pouring off the Lord's body. The dark of the monsoon is made an overflow of His own colour."},
{q:"In what form did the Lord first appear to Vasudeva and Devakī?",o:["As four-armed, bearing conch, discus, mace and lotus","As an ordinary newborn infant, crying like any other","As a boy of five years, already speaking to them","As a formless light filling the whole prison chamber"],a:0,kv:"38.3",vs:"बाल्यस्पृशाऽपि वपुषा दधुषा विभूतीरुद्यत्किरीटकटकाङ्गदहारभासा । शङ्खारिवारिजगदापरिभासितेन मेघासितेन परिलेसिथ सूतिगेहे ॥३॥",vt:"In a body that, though it wore the touch of infancy, bore the marks of majesty — shining with rising crown, bracelets, armlets and necklace, made resplendent by conch, discus, lotus and mace, and dark as a cloud — You shone in the chamber of birth.",exp:"He was born in the full Viṣṇu form, ornamented and armed, radiant enough to light the cell — and only after His parents had praised Him and been reminded of their former births did He put it away."},
{q:"Why did the Lord withdraw that form?",o:["Because Kaṃsa's guards were waking outside","At His parents' plea, lest it be discovered","Because the hour of the conjunction had passed","To spare Devakī the fear it caused her"],a:1,kv:"38.7",vs:"मात्रा च नेत्रसलिलास्तृतगात्रवल्या स्तोत्रैरभिष्टुतगुणः करुणालयस्त्वम् । प्राचीनजन्मयुगलं प्रतिबोध्य ताभ्यां मातुर्गिरा दधिथ मानुषबालवेषम् ॥७॥",vt:"Your qualities praised in hymns by the mother too, the creepers of whose limbs were covered over with the water of her eyes, You who are the abode of compassion, having made those two recall their two former births, at the mother's word took on the guise of a human child.",exp:"Devakī begged Him to hide the four-armed shape, knowing what Kaṃsa would do if word of it reached him. He became an ordinary infant — and it is in that helpless form that He is carried out of the prison."}
]},
{n:39,t:"Story of Yogamaya",kv:"39.1",bh:"Bhāgavata X.3–4",
vt:"Carrying You, that foremost of the Yadu race went out and saw the daughter of Kalinda, her waters risen sky-high. And behold — that vast gathering of water, like a flood conjured up by magic, in that very instant became measurable by the front of the foot.",
vs:"भवन्तमयमुद्वहन् यदुकुलोद्वहो निस्सरन् ददर्श गगनोच्चलज्जलभरां कलिन्दात्मजाम् । अहो सलिलसञ्चयः स पुनरैन्द्रजालोदितो जलौघ इव तत्क्षणात् प्रपदमेयतामाययौ ॥",
qs:[
{q:"What happened to the Yamunā as Vasudeva carried the child across?",o:["It parted into two walls of standing water","It froze over and bore them on its surface","Its risen flood sank to the depth of a footstep","It bore them downstream to Gokula itself"],a:2,exp:"'Prapada-meyatām āyayau' — measurable by the fore part of the foot. The verse first raises the river sky-high, then reduces it in an instant, and calls the whole thing 'aindrajālodita', conjured like a magician's trick."},
{q:"What did Vasudeva do on reaching the cowherd settlement?",o:["Woke Yaśodā and told her everything","Left the child at the door and turned back","Waited until morning to give up the child","Laid the child down and took the girl in exchange"],a:3,kv:"39.2",vs:"प्रसुप्तपशुपालिकां निभृतमारुदद्बालिकामपावृतकवाटिकां पशुपवाटिकामाविशन् । भवन्तमयमर्पयन् प्रसवतल्पके तत्पदाद्वहन् कपटकन्यकां स्वपुरमागतो वेगतः ॥२॥",vt:"Entering the cowherds' enclosure — where the herdswomen slept, where the girl-child cried softly, and the door stood unbarred — he laid You upon the bed of birth, and carrying from that place the girl who was a deception, came swiftly back to his own city.",exp:"'Bhavantam ayam arpayan prasava-talpake tat-padād vahan kapaṭa-kanyakām' — laying You on the bed of birth and lifting from that place the girl who was a deception. The herdswomen slept, the door stood unbarred, and he was back before Mathurā stirred."},
{q:"What did the exchanged girl do when Kaṃsa tried to kill her?",o:["She rose skyward and told him his slayer lived","She vanished from his grasp without a word","She struck him down where he stood","She wept, and Kaṃsa relented for a time"],a:0,kv:"39.6",vs:"नृशंसतर कंस ते किमु मया विनिष्पिष्टया बभूव भवदन्तकः क्वचन चिन्त्यतां ते हितम् । इति त्वदनुजा विभो खलमुदीर्य तं जग्मुषी मरुद्गणपणायिता भुवि च मन्दिराण्येयुषी ॥६॥",vt:"'Most cruel Kaṃsa, what use to you is my being dashed down? He who is to be your end has come to be somewhere. Consider what is good for you' — so Your sister spoke to that wicked one, and going her way, honoured by the host of winds, came to be in shrines upon the earth.",exp:"Yogamāyā slipped from his hands, rose into the air in her eight-armed form, and told him the one who would kill him was born elsewhere and already beyond his reach — leaving him to rage at an empty room."}
]},
{n:40,t:"Salvation of Putana",kv:"40.1",bh:"Bhāgavata X.5–6",
vt:"Thereafter, seeing Nanda — that abode of abundant auspiciousness — come to the royal city for the paying of tribute, Your father, who knew of the doings of Kaṃsa's helpers, spoke to him.",
vs:"तदनु नन्दममन्दशुभास्पदं नृपपुरीं करदानकृते गतम् । समवलोक्य जगाद भवत्पिता विदितकंससहायजनोद्यमः ॥",
qs:[
{q:"Why had Nanda come to the royal city?",o:["To present the child before the king","For the paying of tribute that was due","To seek justice against Kaṃsa's officers","To consult the astrologers of the court"],a:1,exp:"'Kara-dāna-kṛte gatam' — come for the giving of tax. Vasudeva, knowing what Kaṃsa's agents were about, uses the ordinary errand to warn him and send him home quickly."},
{q:"In what guise did Pūtanā come to Gokula?",o:["As an old nurse seeking work in the house","As a bird settling on the roof of the cot","As a beautiful woman offering her breast","As a cowherd girl come to see the child"],a:2,kv:"40.4",vs:"अवसरे खलु तत्र च काचन व्रजपदे मधुराकृतिरङ्गना । तरलषट्पदलालितकुन्तला कपटपोतक ते निकटं गता ॥४॥",vt:"Just then, in the cowherds' settlement, a certain woman of sweet form, her locks caressed by hovering bees, came near to You, the deceiving child.",exp:"She took a lovely form and walked in unchallenged, the poison hidden on her breast. The infant took hold and drew out her life along with the milk, and she fell in her own vast demonic shape across the pasture."},
{q:"What happened as the child drew at the poisoned breast?",o:["She snatched him away and fled into the night","The poison had no effect and she withdrew quietly","The cowherd women pulled him free in time","He drew out her life with the milk, and she fell"],a:3,kv:"40.8",vs:"असुभिरेव समं धयति त्वयि स्तनमसौ स्तनितोपमनिस्वना । निरपतद्भयदायि निजं वपुः प्रतिगता प्रविसार्य भुजावुभौ ॥८॥",vt:"As You drank at her breast, drinking her very life along with it, she, with a cry like a thunderclap, flinging out both arms, fell back in her own terrifying shape.",exp:"'Asubhir eva samaṁ dhayati tvayi stanam' — You drank her life along with the milk; with a cry like a thunderclap, flinging out both arms, she fell back in her own terrifying shape. Bhattathiri does not describe the burning of her body or the destiny given her in the fuller Bhāgavata account."}
]},
{n:41,t:"Cremation of Putana",kv:"41.1",bh:"Bhāgavata X.6",
vt:"The lords of Vraja, having heard Vasudeva's words, came back along the road with frightened hearts; and seeing a certain thing that had crushed every tree beneath it, they took refuge in You.",
vs:"व्रजेश्वरैः शौरिवचो निशम्य समाव्रजन्नध्वनि भीतचेताः । निष्पिष्टनिश्शेषतरुं निरीक्ष्य कञ्चित्पदार्थं शरणं गतस्वाम् ॥१॥",
qs:[
{q:"What did the cowherds do with Pūtanā's body?",o:["Cut it up with axes and cast it far away","Left it where it lay and moved the settlement","Buried it at the boundary of the village","Carried it to the Yamunā and sank it there"],a:0,kv:"41.2",vs:"निशम्य गोपीवचनादुदन्तं सर्वेऽपि गोपा भयविस्मयान्धाः । त्वत्पातितं घोरपिशाचदेहं देहुर्विदूरेऽथ कुठारकृत्तम् ॥२॥",vt:"Hearing the account from the cowherd women, all the cowherds too, blinded by fear and astonishment, cut up with axes the dreadful ghoul-body You had felled, and threw it far away.",exp:"'Kuṭhāra-kṛttam' — blinded by fear and astonishment, they hewed the ghoul-body apart and threw it well away from Vraja before burning it."},
{q:"Why did the smoke of her burning smell sweet?",o:["The cowherds had heaped sandalwood on it","Because the Lord had drunk at her breast","Because she had repented in her last moment","The gods had rained fragrance from the sky"],a:1,kv:"41.4",vs:"मदङ्गसङ्गस्य फलं न दूरे क्षणेन तावत् भवतामपि स्यात् । इत्युल्लपन् वल्लवतल्लजेभ्यः त्वं पूतनामातनुथाः सुगन्धिम् ॥४॥",vt:"'The fruit of contact with My body is not far off — in a moment it shall be yours also': saying this, as it were, to the best of the cowherds, You made Pūtanā fragrant.",exp:"'Mad-aṅga-saṅgasya phalaṁ na dūre' — the fruit of contact with My body is not far off. She came to kill and gave her breast; that touch alone earned her a fragrance the cowherds mistook for agaru and sandal."},
{q:"To what does Dasakam 41 compare the child passed among the women?",o:["A jewel carried from hand to hand","A lamp borne through a darkened house","A bee upon a garland of reddening lotuses","A cloud drifting between two mountains"],a:2,kv:"41.9",vs:"भवद्वपुःस्पर्शनकौतुकेन करात्करं गोपवधूजनेन । नीतस्त्वमाताम्रसरोजमालाव्यालम्बिलोलम्बतुलामलासीः ॥९॥",vt:"Passed from hand to hand by the cowherd women in their eagerness to touch Your body, You came to look like a hovering bee upon a garland of reddening lotuses.",exp:"Eager to touch Him, the cowherd women passed Him from hand to hand — and He looked, Bhattathiri says, like a hovering bee on a garland of reddish lotuses, their palms being the flowers."}
]},
{n:42,t:"Destruction of the Cart-Wheel Asura",kv:"42.1",bh:"Bhāgavata X.7",
vt:"Once, O Lord, on the day of Your birth-star, when kinswomen and brāhmaṇas had been invited, the queen of Vraja set You down near the cart and busied herself in the kitchen and elsewhere.",
vs:"कदापि जन्मर्क्षदिने तव प्रभो निमन्त्रितज्ञातिवधूमहीसुरा । महानसस्त्वां सविधे निधाय सा महानसादौ ववृते व्रजेश्वरी ॥१॥",
qs:[
{q:"How did the boys say the cart was overturned?",o:["A gust of wind had lifted and dropped it","One of them had leaned against the wheel","An unseen hand had pushed it from behind","The crying child struck it with his foot"],a:3,kv:"42.6",vs:"कुमारकस्यास्य पयोधरार्थिनः प्ररोदने लोलपदाम्बुजाहतम् । मया मया दृष्टमनो विपर्यगादितीश ते पालकबालका जगुः ॥६॥",vt:"'This little boy, wanting the breast, struck it in his crying with his restless lotus foot, and it overturned — I saw it, I saw it!' So, O Lord, the boys who watched over You declared.",exp:"'Lola-padāmbujāhatam — mayā mayā dṛṣṭam' — I saw it, I saw it! Wanting the breast and crying, He struck it with His restless lotus foot. The children reported plainly what the elders could not credit."},
{q:"How did the elders respond to the wrecked cart?",o:["They stood bewildered, fingers on their noses","They accused the children of carelessness","They called at once for a priest to divine it","They set about repairing it without a word"],a:0,kv:"42.5",vs:"कस्को नु कौतस्कुत एष विस्मयो विशङ्कटं यच्छकटं विपाटितम् । न कारणं किञ्चिदिहेति ते स्थिताः स्वनासिकादत्तकरास्त्वदीक्षकाः ॥५॥",vt:"'Who is this? Whence has he come? What a wonder, that this huge cart lies broken apart! There is no cause for it here at all' — so they stood, fingers laid on their noses, gazing at You.",exp:"'Kasko nu kautaskuta eṣa vismayaḥ' — who is this, whence has he come, what wonder is this? Seeing no cause for so huge a cart to be split apart, they stood with fingers laid on their noses, staring at the child."},
{q:"What did they conclude had become of the demon in the cart?",o:["That he had fled invisibly to Mathurā","That he was absorbed into the Lord Himself","That the gods had carried his body away","That he had never been in the cart at all"],a:1,kv:"42.10",vs:"अनोनिलीनः किल हन्तुमागतः सुरारिरेवं भवता विहिंसितः । रजोऽपि नो दृष्टममुष्य तत्कथं स शुद्धसत्त्वे त्वयि लीनवान् ध्रुवम् ॥१०॥",vt:"A demon hidden in the cart had surely come to kill, and was destroyed by You in this way. Yet not even his dust was seen — how so? Assuredly he was absorbed into You, who are pure being.",exp:"'Sa śuddha-sattve tvayi līnavān dhruvam' — not even his dust was found. He must, they say, have been absorbed into You, who are pure being. Coming to kill, he was given the end that devotees are given."}
]},
{n:43,t:"Destruction of Trinavarta",kv:"43.1",bh:"Bhāgavata X.7",
vt:"One day, O Lord of Guruvāyūr, unable to carry You who had taken on a weight grown suddenly immense, Your mother laid You on the bed and, wondering 'what can this be?', moved about the house with a suspicion in her.",
vs:"त्वामेकदा गुरुमरुत्पुरनाथ वोढुं गाढाधिरूढगरिमाणमपारयन्ती । माता निधाय शयने किमिदं बतेति ध्यायन्त्यचेष्टत गृहेषु निविष्टशङ्का ॥१॥",
qs:[
{q:"Why did Yaśodā set the child down before the whirlwind came?",o:["She was called away to the milking shed","He had fallen asleep against her shoulder","He had grown suddenly too heavy to hold","The other women had asked to hold Him"],a:2,kv:"43.1",vs:"त्वामेकदा गुरुमरुत्पुरनाथ वोढुं गाढाधिरूढगरिमाणमपारयन्ती । माता निधाय शयने किमिदं बतेति ध्यायन्त्यचेष्टत गृहेषु निविष्टशङ्का ॥१॥",vt:"One day, O Lord of Guruvāyūr, unable to carry You who had taken on a weight grown suddenly immense, Your mother laid You on the bed and, wondering 'what can this be?', moved about the house with a suspicion in her.",exp:"'Gāḍhādhirūḍha-garimāṇam apārayantī' — unable to bear a weight that had grown immense. She laid Him on the bed wondering what it could mean, and went about the house with a suspicion in her."},
{q:"How did Tṛṇāvarta come to fall out of the sky?",o:["The Lord struck him between the shoulders","Balarāma brought him down with a stone","The wind failed him above the settlement","He sought release and the child would not let go"],a:3,kv:"43.5",vs:"रोदोपकर्णनवशादुपगम्य गेहं क्रन्दत्सु नन्दमुखगोपकुलेषु दीनः । त्वां दानवस्त्वखिलमुक्तिकरं मुमुक्षुस्त्वय्यप्रमुञ्चति पपात वियत्प्रदेशात् ॥५॥",vt:"Hearing the weeping and coming to the house, while Nanda and the cowherds cried out, the demon in his wretchedness longed to be released from You, the giver of release to all; and as You would not let go, he fell from the region of the sky.",exp:"'Tvayy apramuñcati papāta viyat-pradeśāt' — the demon, who had seized Him, now longed to be rid of Him; but the giver of release to all would not release, and the weight bore him down."},
{q:"How was the child found after the demon fell?",o:["Laughing on the demon's chest, unhurt","Asleep among the branches of a tree","Crying at the edge of the settlement","Held in the arms of a passing sage"],a:0,kv:"43.6",vs:"रोदाकुलास्तदनु गोपगणा बहिष्ठपाषाणपृष्ठभुवि देहमतिस्थविष्ठम् । प्रैक्षन्त हन्त निपतन्तममुष्य वक्षस्यक्षीणमेव च भवन्तमलं हसन्तम् ॥६॥",vt:"Then the cowherds, distraught with weeping, saw on the surface of a rock outside that huge and heavy body fallen — and on its breast, unharmed and laughing outright, You.",exp:"'Vakṣasy akṣīṇam eva ca bhavantam alaṁ hasantam' — undiminished and laughing outright on the breast of the huge body smashed upon the rock. They lifted Him away, Bhattathiri says, like a sapphire off a mountain."}
]},
{n:44,t:"Naming Ceremony",kv:"44.1",bh:"Bhāgavata X.8",
vt:"To perform in secret, at Vasudeva's word, the sacraments of You who are without action, the sage Garga, who held the truth of the hours in his heart, came to Your house, O Lord.",
vs:"गूढं वसुदेवगिरा कर्तुं ते निष्क्रियस्य संस्कारान् । हृद्गतहोरातत्त्वो गर्गमुनिस्त्वत् गृहं विभो गतवान् ॥१॥",
qs:[
{q:"What difficulty did Garga voice before naming the child?",o:["That the hour was not astrologically fit","How to name one who has endless names","That Nanda was not the child's true father","That the rite required Vasudeva's presence"],a:1,kv:"44.4",vs:"कथमस्य नाम कुर्वे सहस्रनाम्नो ह्यनन्तनाम्नो वा । इति नूनं गर्गमुनिश्चक्रे तव नाम नाम रहसि विभो ॥४॥",vt:"'How shall I make a name for Him who has a thousand names, or rather endless names?' Surely with this thought the sage Garga made Your name — in secret, O Lord.",exp:"'Katham asya nāma kurve sahasra-nāmno hy ananta-nāmno vā' — how shall I make a name for Him who has a thousand names, or rather no end of them? Bhattathiri lets the naming rest on that difficulty."},
{q:"How does Dasakam 44 explain the name Kṛṣṇa?",o:["From the dark colour of the child's body","From the name of an ancestor of the line","From the root kṛṣ and the syllable ṇa","From the star under which He was born"],a:2,kv:"44.5",vs:"कृषिधातुणकाराभ्यां सत्तानन्दात्मतां किलाभिलपत् । जगदघकर्षित्वं वा कथयदृषिः कृष्णनाम ते व्यतनोत् ॥५॥",vt:"Declaring, by the root kṛṣ and the syllable ṇa, that You are of the nature of being and bliss — or telling that You draw away the sin of the world — the sage set forth Your name as Kṛṣṇa.",exp:"'Kṛṣi-dhātu-ṇa-kārābhyām' — by the root kṛṣ with the syllable ṇa: being and bliss. Or else, the sage says, from His drawing away the sin of the world. Two derivations are offered and neither is chosen."},
{q:"What did Garga stop short of saying about the child?",o:["That he was born to Devakī, not Yaśodā","That Kaṃsa would seek to kill him","That he would leave Vraja before long","That he is Hari Himself"],a:3,kv:"44.9",vs:"अमुनैव सर्वदुर्गं तरितास्थ कृतास्थमत्र तिष्ठध्वम् । हरिरेवेत्यनभिलपन्नित्यादि त्वामवर्णयत् स मुनिः ॥९॥",vt:"'By him alone you will cross every difficulty; abide here with confidence in that' — thus and so the sage described You, without once saying outright, 'he is Hari himself'.",exp:"'Harir evety anabhilapan' — he described the child's greatness at length, said that by him every difficulty would be crossed, and never once said outright, 'he is Hari'. The secrecy was Vasudeva's instruction."}
]},
{n:45,t:"Childhood Pranks of Krishna",kv:"45.1",bh:"Bhāgavata X.8",
vt:"O Murāri, together with Bala — the two of You, adorning the parts of the house with the going of hands and knees, Your lotus feet astir, delighting in the sound of the sweet anklets, moved with a charming swiftness.",
vs:"अयि सबल मुरारे पाणिजानुप्रचारैः किमपि भवनभागान् भूषयन्तौ भवन्तौ । चलितचरणकञ्जौ मञ्जुमञ्जीरशिञ्जाश्रवणकुतुकभाजौ चेरतुश्चारुवेगात् ॥१॥",
qs:[
{q:"Why, in Dasakam 45, does the Lord steal the butter rather than ask?",o:["Having begged of Bali, He would not beg of women","Because the women had refused Him once before","Because Yaśodā had forbidden Him to ask for it","Because the other boys had dared Him to try it"],a:0,kv:"45.9",vs:"मम खलु बलिगेहे याचनं जातमास्तामिह पुनरबलानामग्रतो नैव कुर्वे । इति विहितमतिः किं देव सन्त्यज्य याच्ञां दधिघृतमहरस्त्वं चारुणा चोरणेन ॥९॥",vt:"'In Bali's house I did indeed beg; let that be. Here, before women, I shall not do it' — with this resolve, O Lord, giving up asking, did You take the curd and the ghee by a beautiful stealing?",exp:"'Mama khalu bali-gehe yācanaṁ jātam āstām' — in Bali's house I did indeed beg; let that be. Here, before women, I shall not. So, giving up asking, He took the curd and ghee by a beautiful stealing."},
{q:"What did the thefts produce in the women of the settlement?",o:["A complaint carried to Nanda himself","Neither anger nor grief, for He stole their hearts","A watch set on the storerooms each morning","A quarrel among the households of Vraja"],a:1,kv:"45.10",vs:"तव दधिघृतमोषे घोषयोषाजनानामभजत हृदि रोषो नावकाशं न शोकः । हृदयमपि मुषित्वा हर्षसिन्धौ न्यधास्त्वं स मम शमय रोगान् वातगेहाधिनाथ ॥१०॥",vt:"At Your theft of curd and ghee, neither anger nor grief found any room in the hearts of the women of the settlement; for having stolen their hearts too, You set them in an ocean of joy.",exp:"'Hṛdayam api muṣitvā harṣa-sindhau nyadhās tvam' — having stolen their hearts as well, You set them in an ocean of joy. Anger, Bhattathiri says, found no room in them to occupy."},
{q:"What followed when the child asked for the moon as a fruit?",o:["His mother distracted Him with a lighted lamp","Balarāma explained that it was far away in the sky","At His parents' word He reached up and it came","He wept until the request was quietly forgotten"],a:2,kv:"45.11",vs:"शाखाग्रे विधुं विलोक्य फलमित्य्म्बां च तातं मुहुः संप्रार्थ्याथ तदा तदीयवचसा प्रोत्क्षिप्तबाहौ त्वयि । चित्रं देव शशी स ते कर्मगात् किं ब्रूमहे संपतः ज्योतिर्मण्डलपूरिताखिलवपुः प्रागा विराड्रूपताम् ॥११॥",vt:"Seeing the moon at the tip of a branch and thinking it a fruit, You entreated Your mother and father again and again; and then, at their word, as Your arms were flung up — wonder, O Lord! — that moon came to Your hand. What shall we say? Your whole body, filled at once with the circle of the lights, went forth into the form of the Virāṭ.",exp:"'Citraṁ deva śaśī sa te kara-gāt' — a wonder: the moon came to His hand. And at once His whole body, filled with the circle of the lights, went forth into the form of the Virāṭ, and Nanda was plunged a moment in the ocean of Brahman."}
]},
{n:46,t:"Revelation of the Cosmic Form",kv:"46.1",bh:"Bhāgavata X.8",
vt:"O Lord, once, when You lay upon Your back at the breast and Your mouth fell open in a yawn, the cowherd woman beheld the universe in it.",
vs:"अयि देव पुरा किल त्वयि स्वयमुत्तानशये स्तनन्धये । परिजृम्भणतो व्यपावृते वदने विश्वमचष्ट वल्लवी ॥१॥",
qs:[
{q:"What was the irony in Yaśodā's fear when told He had eaten mud?",o:["She had given Him earth to play with herself","She had eaten mud as a child and been unharmed","The mud was from the bank of the Yamunā","He who devours the worlds might sicken from mud"],a:3,kv:"46.3",vs:"अयि ते प्रलयावधौ विभो क्षितितोयादिसमस्तभक्षिणः । मृदुपाशनतो रुजा भवेदिति भीता जननी चुकोप सा ॥३॥",vt:"'Alas, You who at the end of the aeon devour earth and water and all things — from eating mud there may come sickness to You': so, in fear, that mother of Yours grew angry.",exp:"'Pralayāvadhau kṣiti-toyādi-samasta-bhakṣiṇaḥ' — You who at the end of the aeon devour earth and water and all things. From a mouthful of mud, she feared, some sickness might come to Him."},
{q:"Besides the worlds, what did Yaśodā see in the open mouth?",o:["Her own village, and her own child","The Vedas standing in visible form","The gods assembled in their thousands","Her own face reflected back at her"],a:0,kv:"46.8",vs:"कलशाम्बुधिशायिनं पुनः परवैकुण्ठपदाधिवासिनम् । स्वपुरश्च निजार्भकात्मकं कतिधा त्वां न ददर्श सा मुखे ॥८॥",vt:"You lying on the ocean of milk; You dwelling again in the supreme abode of Vaikuṇṭha; and her own village, and her own child — in how many forms did she not see You in that mouth?",exp:"'Sva-puraṁ ca nijārbhakātmakam' — the milk ocean and Vaikuṇṭha, and then her own settlement, and her own child within it. The next verse opens that regress again inside itself, without end."},
{q:"How does Dasakam 46 end?",o:["She told the whole village what she had seen","He deluded her again and asked for the breast","She fell unconscious and had to be revived","Nanda arrived and the vision was broken"],a:1,kv:"46.10",vs:"धृततत्त्वधियं तदा क्षणं जननीं तां प्रणयेन मोहयन् । स्तनमम्ब दिशेत्युपासजन् भगवन्नद्भुतबाल पाहि माम् ॥१०॥",vt:"Then, deluding by love, for a moment, that mother of Yours who had grasped the truth, You clung to her, saying 'Mother, give me the breast' — O Lord, O wonderful child, protect me.",exp:"'Stanam amba diśety upāsajan' — 'Mother, give me the breast.' She had held the truth for a moment; He covered it over again with love. Bhattathiri leaves the deliberate withdrawal of the vision as the point."}
]},
{n:47,t:"Bound to the Mortar",kv:"47.1",bh:"Bhāgavata X.9",
vt:"One day You came near Your mother as she was churning curd, and stopping her in Your greed for the breast, climbed into her lap and drank at her.",
vs:"एकदा दधिविमाथकारिणीं मातरं समुपसेदिवान् भवान् । स्तन्यलोलुपतया निवारयन्नङ्कमेत्य पपिवान् पयोधरौ ॥१॥",
qs:[
{q:"Why did the child break the pot of curd?",o:["It stood in the way of his crawling","To distract His mother from the churning","In anger at His drinking being interrupted","The other boys had asked Him to do it"],a:2,kv:"47.3",vs:"सामिपीतरसभङ्गसङ्गतक्रोधभारपरिभूतचेतसा । मन्थदण्डमुपगृह्य पाटितं हन्त देव दधिभाजनं त्वया ॥३॥",vt:"His mind overcome by the weight of anger at the breaking of the half-drunk draught, He took hold of the churning rod — and alas, O Lord, the vessel of curd was split by You.",exp:"'Sāmi-pīta-rasa-bhaṅga-saṅgata-krodha' — the milk on the fire boiled over, she set Him down half-fed and hurried to it, and the anger at that interruption took hold of the churning rod."},
{q:"What happened when Yaśodā tried to bind Him?",o:["The cords burned away in her hands","He slipped free as often as she tied Him","The knots came undone of themselves","Every cord fell short by two fingers"],a:3,kv:"47.7",vs:"बन्धुमिच्छति यमेव सज्जनस्तं भवन्तमयि बन्धुमिच्छती । सा नियुज्य रशनागुणान् बहून् द्व्यङ्गुलोनमखिलं किलैक्षत ॥७॥",vt:"She who wished to bind Him whom good people wish to have as their kinsman, joining many cords together, saw the whole of it fall short, every time, by the breadth of two fingers.",exp:"'Dvy-aṅgulonam akhilaṁ kilaikṣata' — she joined cord to cord and every time the whole length fell short by the breadth of two fingers. Bhattathiri notes the turn: she wished to bind the one whom the good wish to have as kin."},
{q:"Why was He bound in the end?",o:["Out of compassion, seeing her worn out","Her friends held Him while she tied the knot","Balarāma persuaded Him to submit","The cords were blessed by a sage"],a:0,kv:"47.8",vs:"विस्मितोत्स्मितसखीजनेक्षितां स्विन्नसन्नवपुषं निरीक्ष्य ताम् । नित्यमुक्तवपुरप्यहो हरे बन्धमेव कृपयाऽन्वमन्यथाः ॥८॥",vt:"Seeing her, gazed at by her friends who smiled in astonishment, her body drooping and wet with sweat — though Your form is eternally free, ah Hari, out of compassion You consented to the very binding.",exp:"'Nitya-mukta-vapur apy aho hare bandham eva kṛpayā anvamanyathāḥ' — though His form is eternally free, seeing her drooping and wet with sweat before her smiling friends, He consented to the binding."}
]},
{n:48,t:"Release of Nala-Kubara and Manigriva",kv:"48.1",bh:"Bhāgavata X.10",
vt:"Praised joyfully by the host of gods in their great gladness, who cried out 'Dāmodara!' — Your small belly caught upon the mortar, You saw at no great distance two arjuna trees.",
vs:"मुदा सुरौघैस्त्वमुदारसम्मदैरुदीर्य दामोदर इत्यभिष्टुतः । मृदुदरः स्वैरमुलूखले लगन्नदूरतो द्वौ ककुभावुदैक्षथाः ॥१॥",
qs:[
{q:"Who were the two trees, before they were trees?",o:["Two gandharvas cursed by Durvāsas","Kubera's sons, insolent with wealth","Two brothers who had robbed a sacrifice","Servants of Yama sent to fetch a soul"],a:1,kv:"48.2",vs:"कुबेरसूनुर्नलकूबराभिधः परो मणिग्रीव इति प्रथां गतः । महेशसेवाधिगतश्रियोन्मदौ चिरं किल त्वद्विमुखावखेलताम् ॥२॥",vt:"Kubera's son named Nalakūbara, and the other who had come to be known as Maṇigrīva — the two of them, made insolent by the wealth got through service to Maheśa, sported long, averse to You.",exp:"'Kubera-sūnur nalakūbarābhidhaḥ paro maṇigrīva' — made insolent by the wealth got through serving Maheśa, they had long sported averse to the Lord. Nārada found them drunk and naked in the Gaṅgā."},
{q:"What form did Nārada's curse take?",o:["To wander the earth for an age without memory","To be born as demons for three lives in turn","To stand as trees, then be freed on seeing Hari","To lose their wealth and serve among cowherds"],a:2,kv:"48.5",vs:"युवामवाप्तौ ककुभात्मतां चिरं हरिं निरीक्ष्याथ पदं स्वमाप्नुतम् । इतीरेतौ तौ भवदीक्षणस्पृहां गतौ व्रजान्ते ककुभौ बभूवतुः ॥५॥",vt:"'Having become trees for a long while, you shall behold Hari, and then attain your own station' — thus addressed, those two, come to long for the sight of You, became two arjuna trees at the edge of Vraja.",exp:"'Yuvām avāptau kakubhātmatāṁ ciraṁ hariṁ nirīkṣyātha padaṁ svam āpnutam' — the curse and the release were spoken in one breath. They came, longing for the sight of Him, to stand as two arjuna trees at the edge of Vraja."},
{q:"Who untied the child after the trees came down?",o:["Yaśodā, once her anger had passed","Balarāma, before the cowherds arrived","The two Yakṣas, in gratitude","Nanda, at a look from the abashed mother"],a:3,kv:"48.9",vs:"ततस्तरूद्दारणदारुणारवप्रकम्पिसम्पातिनि गोपमण्डले । विलज्जितत्वज्जननीमुखेक्षिणा व्यमोक्षि नन्देन भवान् विमोक्षदः ॥९॥",vt:"Then, as the cowherd company came running, shaken by the terrible noise of the trees being torn apart, You who are the giver of release were released by Nanda, at a look from Your abashed mother's face.",exp:"'Vyamokṣi nandena bhavān vimokṣadaḥ' — He who is the giver of release was released by Nanda. Bhattathiri sets the two words side by side and leaves them there."}
]},
{n:49,t:"Trek to Vrindavana",kv:"49.1",bh:"Bhāgavata X.11",
vt:"The cowherds, who did not know Your power, suspected the falling of the trees and the rest in that settlement to be a train of portents without cause, and set their minds on going elsewhere.",
vs:"भवत्प्रभावाविदुरा हि गोपास्तरुप्रपातादिकमत्र गोष्ठे । अहेतुमुत्पातगणं विशङ्क्य प्रयातुमन्यत्र मनो वितेनुः ॥१॥",
qs:[
{q:"Why did the cowherds decide to leave the settlement?",o:["They read the strange events as portents","The pasture there had been grazed bare","Kaṃsa's men had been seen nearby","A sage had advised them to move on"],a:0,kv:"49.1",vs:"भवत्प्रभावाविदुरा हि गोपास्तरुप्रपातादिकमत्र गोष्ठे । अहेतुमुत्पातगणं विशङ्क्य प्रयातुमन्यत्र मनो वितेनुः ॥१॥",vt:"The cowherds, who did not know Your power, suspected the falling of the trees and the rest in that settlement to be a train of portents without cause, and set their minds on going elsewhere.",exp:"'Bhavat-prabhāvāvidurā hi gopāḥ' — not knowing Your power, they took the falling of the trees and the rest for a train of omens without cause, and resolved to go elsewhere."},
{q:"Who proposed Vṛndāvana, and at whose prompting?",o:["Nanda, on the advice of the brāhmaṇas there","Upananda — at the Lord's own prompting","Garga, before he departed from the house","Balarāma, who had seen the wood before"],a:1,kv:"49.2",vs:"तत्रोपनन्दाभिधगोपवर्यो जगौ भवत्प्रेरणयैव नूनम् । इतः प्रतीच्यां विपिनं मनोज्ञं वृन्दावनं नाम विराजतीति ॥२॥",vt:"Thereupon the foremost of the cowherds, named Upananda, said — surely at Your own prompting — 'from here, to the west, there shines a delightful wood by the name of Vṛndāvana'.",exp:"'Jagau bhavat-preraṇayaiva nūnam' — surely it was by Your own prompting. The cowherds thought they were fleeing portents; the move was the Lord bringing Himself to the wood His play belongs to."},
{q:"What kept the women from noticing the length of the road?",o:["The songs the cowherds sang as they walked","The beauty of the country they passed through","Drinking in the syllables of His playful talk","The speed of the wagons over level ground"],a:2,kv:"49.4",vs:"अनोमनोज्ञध्वनिधेनुपालीखुरप्रणादान्तरतो वधूभिः । भवद्विनोदालपिताक्षराणि प्रपीय नाज्ञायत मार्गदैर्घ्यम् ॥४॥",vt:"Amid the pleasant creaking of the wagons and the sound of the hooves of the herds of cows, drinking in the syllables of Your playful talk, the women did not notice the length of the road.",exp:"'Bhavad-vinodālapitākṣarāṇi prapīya nājñāyata mārga-dairghyam' — drinking in the syllables of Your playful talk, amid the creak of the wagons and the hooves of the cows, they did not know how far they had come."}
]},
{n:50,t:"Calf-demon and Stork-Demon",kv:"50.1",bh:"Bhāgavata X.11",
vt:"Then, in lovely Vṛndāvana with its restless swarms of bees, together with the cowherd boys and eager for the tending of the calves, O Lord, O glorious one, You wandered with the bearer of the plough for Your companion, holding the buffalo-horn, the flute and the staff, Your body's radiance delighting the eye.",
vs:"तरलमधुकृत् वृन्दे वृन्दावनेऽथ मनोहरे पशुपशिशुभिः साकं वत्सानुपालनलोलुपः । हलधरसखो देव श्रीमन् विचेरिथ धारयन् गवलमुरलीवेत्रं नेत्राभिरामतनुद्युतिः ॥१॥",
qs:[
{q:"How did the Lord kill the demon in the form of a calf?",o:["He pierced it with the sharp point of His staff","He struck it once between the horns and it fell","He drove it into the Yamunā and it was drowned","He seized its leg, whirled it, and flung it away"],a:3,kv:"50.4",vs:"रभसविलसत्पुच्छं विच्छायतोऽस्य विलोकयन् किमपि वलितस्कन्धं रन्ध्रप्रतीक्षमुदीक्षितम् । तमथ चरणे बिभ्रद्विभ्रामयन् मुहुरुच्चकैः कुहचन महावृक्षे चिक्षेपिथ क्षतजीवितम् ॥४॥",vt:"Watching it from the shade — its tail flourishing rapidly, its shoulder somehow turned, its gaze waiting for an opening — You then took it by the leg, whirled it aloft again and again, and flung it, its life destroyed, into some great tree.",exp:"'Caraṇe bibhrad vibhrāmayan muhur uccakaiḥ' — He had watched it from the shade, tail flourishing and shoulder turned, waiting for an opening; then took it by the leg, whirled it high, and threw it lifeless into a great tree."},
{q:"How did the child explain the flowers falling on His head?",o:["That the demon's fall had shaken them loose","That the gods were honouring the cowherd boys","That a wind had carried them from Vṛndāvana","He gave no answer at all and walked on ahead"],a:0,kv:"50.6",vs:"सुरभिलतमा मूर्धन्यूर्ध्वं कुतः कुसुमावली निपतति तवेत्युक्तो बालैः सहेलमुदैरयः । झटिति दनुजक्षेपेणोर्ध्वं गतस्तरुमण्डलात् कुसुमनिकरः सोऽयं नूनं समेति शनैरिति ॥६॥",vt:"Asked by the boys, 'whence does this most fragrant row of flowers fall upon your head from above?', You said playfully: 'this mass of blossom, thrown upward from the circle of the trees by the flinging of the demon, is surely coming down again slowly.'",exp:"The gods had rained them from the sky, and the boys asked where they came from. He answered playfully that the demon, flung upward, had thrown the blossom up out of the trees, and it was only now drifting down again."},
{q:"How was Bakāsura killed?",o:["He was struck down as he flew overhead","He swallowed the Lord and had his beak torn apart","He was caught fast in the coils of Kāliya","He fled and was pursued down to the Yamunā"],a:1,kv:"50.8",vs:"पिबति सलिलं गोपव्राते भवन्तमभिद्रुतः स किल निगिलन्नग्निप्रख्यं पुनर्द्रुतमुद्वमन् । दलयितुमगात्त्रोट्याः कोट्या तदाऽऽशु भवान् विभो खलजनभिदाचुञ्चुश्चञ्चू प्रगृह्य ददार तम् ॥८॥",vt:"As the company of cowherds drank the water, that one rushed upon You, and swallowing You — like fire — quickly vomited You out again; and when he came to split You with the sharp point of his beak, then at once, O Lord, You who are renowned for the breaking of the wicked seized the beak and tore him apart.",exp:"'Cañcūṁ pragṛhya dadāra tam' — he swallowed Him, vomited Him out again like fire, and came to split Him with the point of his beak; and the Lord took hold of the beak and tore him in two."}
]}
];

export { DASAKAMS };
